Chapter: Lir
  cites Codex chapter Build Settings (max-recursion-depth)

 The flat low-level IR: the representation on which register allocation
 will happen (design doc `docs/Designs/Active/Compiler/LIR.md`, middle-end
 campaign step 5). The tree `IRExpr` has no linear order and no basic
 blocks, so linear-scan allocation cannot be expressed over it; the LIR is
 that linear order. This chapter is the second increment — it adds basic
 blocks and branches so that `if` lowers, on top of the first increment's
 straight-line whitelist. It is still deliberately dead to the shipping
 compiler: it lowers nothing in the default pipeline, emits no machine
 code, and is reachable only through the opt-in `lir-dump` pass, so the
 seed's own compilation is byte-identical and the fixed point is
 undisturbed. The allocator and the x86 selector are later ratchet steps,
 each its own CL.

 This increment runs over the RESOLVED, LIFTED IR (`cdx-chapter`, after
 the RESOLVE and LIFT phases), not the raw lowering, because a constructor
 pattern needs its scrutinee's `SumTy` -- the constructor list for the tag
 and the field types for the packed offsets -- and RESOLVE is what expands
 a `ConstructedTy` name to that `SumTy`. The straight-line / `if` / scalar
 `match` increments did not need types, so they ran pre-RESOLVE; the
 constructor increment is the first that does, so the `lir-dump` report was
 moved to after RESOLVE (`opening.codex`, driven by the pipeline name but
 executed post-resolve). The eventual allocator and selector consume the
 same post-frontend IR, so this is also their correct home.

 **This increment lowers straight-line code, `if`, scalar `match`, and
 constructor-pattern `match`.** A function whose body is integer literals,
 names, add/sub/mul, let bindings, and `if` expressions lowers to a list of
 basic blocks over virtual registers. An `if` is destination-driven and
 phi-free: a result vreg is allocated, the condition is lowered into the
 current block ending in a conditional branch, each arm is lowered into its
 own block that moves its value into the shared result vreg and jumps to a
 join block, and lowering continues in the join. There are no loops — the
 tree IR has none — so flattening a body yields a DAG and the join is the
 only merge point, exactly as `LIR.md` section 3 specifies. A `match` over
 a scalar scrutinee with literal, variable, and wildcard patterns lowers
 the same way: the scrutinee becomes an operand, and the arms become a
 decision chain of `LiCmp`/branch tests ending in an unconditional
 catch-all. A `match` on **constructor patterns** reuses that decision
 chain with the first LIR memory instruction, `LiLoad`: a tested
 constructor arm loads the variant tag from offset 0 of the scrutinee
 pointer (`LiLoad tag sv 0 w8`), compares it against the constructor's tag,
 and branches; the arm's body first binds each variable field with a
 `LiLoad` at the field's packed offset and width, then lowers the body.
 Every arm still moves its value into one result vreg and jumps to a join.
 Only variable and wildcard sub-patterns are handled -- a nested
 constructor, literal, or vector sub-pattern, a real guard, or a scrutinee
 type that did not resolve to a `SumTy` makes the whole function fall back:
 `lower-def-to-lir` answers "not lowerable" and the tree emitter keeps it.
 Calls, records, effects, and vector patterns still fall back. The
 whitelist grows one construct per CL.

 Alongside the lowering, this chapter now computes **backward liveness** over
 the block DAG (Section: Liveness) -- the input the linear-scan allocator
 needs. It is a single backward sweep with no fixpoint (the DAG has no
 loops), and `-Passes lir-dump` prints it as one `LIVE <def>: bN in {..}`
 line per lowerable function beside the `LIR <def>:` dump.

 It also carries the **allocation verifier** (Section: Allocation Verifier,
 LIR.md section 4): given each vreg an assigned location, it re-derives what
 each location holds along every path and asserts every use reads its vreg
 from that location -- the checker that stands before the allocator so the
 allocator is validated from its first line.

 And it now carries the **linear-scan allocator** itself (Section:
 Allocator, LIR.md section 5: Wimmer 2005 minus splitting, on a DAG). It
 computes each vreg's live interval over a flat instruction numbering,
 lets values with disjoint intervals share a register, and spills a whole
 interval to a stack slot by furthest-next-use (Belady) when registers run
 out. Its `List Location` output is exactly what the allocation verifier
 checks, so every allocation is validated on the spot -- one
 `ALLOC <def>: v0->r0 v1->r0 ... [alloc-check: ok]` line per function, now
 showing register reuse and (under pressure) spill slots. It is still
 analysis only: nothing the compiler emits changes, because the x86
 selector that would consume this allocation is a later ratchet step
 (LIR.md section 8).

Section: Representation

 A virtual register is an integer id. Parameters take ids `0 ..
 nparams-1`; every intermediate value defined by an instruction takes the
 next fresh id. There is no recycling and no allocation here — that is the
 allocator's job, later; lowering only assigns distinct ids.

 An operand is either a virtual register or an immediate. Immediates flow
 without an instruction: `x + 3` lowers to one binary instruction whose
 right operand is the immediate 3, not a move of 3 into a register first.

  LirValue =
   | LvReg (Integer)
   | LvImm (Integer)

 The binary operators this increment lowers as values — the three that
 need no flags register and no idiv: add, subtract, multiply. Division
 (fixed register pair, can trap) arrives with the construct that needs it.

 `LoRem` is the first operator whose registers are not the allocator's to
 choose. Every target that has a divide instruction at all tends to fix them:
 on x86-64 `idiv` divides RDX:RAX by its operand and leaves the remainder in
 RDX, so two registers are spoken for at that instruction whatever else is
 going on. It is here rather than deferred because `gcd` is the only bench the
 selector declines outright, and it declines on `math-mod` -- the leaf inliner
 rewrites that to a remainder, and the whitelist took add, subtract and
 multiply and nothing else. The tail call was never the problem.

  LirOp =
   | LoAdd
   | LoSub
   | LoMul
   | LoRem
   | LoDiv

 The six comparison conditions a branch tests. A comparison sets flags
 (`LiCmp`) and the following conditional branch reads them — the fused
 compare-and-branch shape x86 emits directly. Comparisons appear only in
 an `if` condition in this increment; a comparison in value position (a
 `let`-bound boolean) needs `setcc` materialisation and is deferred.

  LirCond =
   | LcEq
   | LcNe
   | LcLt
   | LcGt
   | LcLe
   | LcGe

 The instructions. `LiBin` computes an arithmetic result into a fresh
 vreg. `LiMove` copies an operand into a vreg — the destination-driven
 join uses it to write the shared result vreg from each arm. `LiLoad`
 reads a value from memory into a fresh vreg: `LiLoad dst base off width
 signed` loads `width` bytes from `[base + off]`, sign- or zero-extending a
 narrow field to 64 bits. It is the first LIR memory instruction, added for
 constructor patterns — the tag load at offset 0 and each variable field
 load at its packed offset. `LiCmp` sets flags from two operands and
 defines nothing. `LiTest` is its sibling: it sets flags from the bitwise
 AND of two operands and likewise defines nothing, which is the whole of
 what `x86`'s `test` does and what a remainder-against-zero needs.
 `LiBranch` and `LiJump` are terminators: a block's last
 instruction transfers control, naming successor blocks by id, never a
 vreg.

 `LiRet` returns an operand from the function. It is a terminator with no
 successor, and it is what lets an `if` in tail position stop building a join
 value: each arm returns its own answer where it computed it, the way the tree
 emitter has always done, instead of every arm moving into one shared vreg
 that then lives from the first arm all the way to a single exit. That shared
 vreg was expensive out of proportion to its size -- a single conservative
 interval spanning every call in the body, which forced it into a callee-saved
 register and cost a push, a pop and a final move.

  LirInsn =
   | LiBin (LirOp) (Integer) (LirValue) (LirValue)
   | LiMove (Integer) (LirValue)
   | LiLoad (Integer) (LirValue) (Integer) (Integer) (Boolean)
   | LiCmp (LirValue) (LirValue)
   | LiTest (LirValue) (LirValue)
   | LiBranch (LirCond) (Integer) (Integer)
   | LiJump (Integer)
   | LiCall (Integer) (Text) (List LirValue)
   | LiRet (LirValue)

 A basic block: an id and its instruction sequence. A block that transfers
 control ends in a terminator (`LiBranch` or `LiJump`) as its last
 instruction; the one exit block ends without a terminator and its final
 value is the function result. Blocks carry dense ids `0 .. nblocks-1`
 assigned in creation order, and creation order is a topological order of
 the DAG — a block is finished before any block it jumps to is built — so
 the verifier and the future allocator can walk `blocks` in list order
 without a separate sort.

  LirBlock = record {
   id : Integer,
   insns : List LirInsn
  }

 A lowered function: its name, how many parameters (which occupy the first
 vregs), the basic blocks, the operand that holds the result, and the
 number of vregs used (the id ceiling, for the allocator to size its
 interval table). A straight-line body is one block with no terminator.

 `rets` says every path leaves through a `LiRet` of its own. The function then
 has no result operand to speak of and no single exit to fall into, so the
 selector emits no trailing result-and-epilogue -- each return carries its own.

  LirFunc = record {
   name : Text,
   nparams : Integer,
   blocks : List LirBlock,
   result : LirValue,
   nvregs : Integer,
   rets : Boolean
  }

 A back-edge is a jump to block 0, and only a back-edge is: block 0 is the
 entry, the header a self-tail-call returns to, and the DAG lowering never
 targets it -- every join it creates takes a fresh id from a counter that
 starts at 1. Its presence is what tells liveness it needs a fixpoint and the
 interval builder that the parameters live around the loop, so a function
 lowered without a self-tail-call has none and every analysis below behaves
 precisely as it did before back-edges existed.

 Comparing ids -- treating a jump to an id at or below the jumping block's own
 id as a back-edge -- looks equivalent and is not. Block ids are handed out
 eagerly, before the arms they belong to are lowered, so a nested `if` numbers
 its own blocks above the enclosing join it eventually jumps to: in `fib` the
 inner join is block 6 and jumps forward to the outer join, block 3. That is a
 perfectly ordinary forward edge in the DAG, and reading it as a back-edge
 extended `fib`'s parameter across the whole body, cost it a callee-saved
 register, and grew it by two instructions. It is the block *list* that is
 topological, not the ids -- which the emitter already knows, and indexes its
 offset table by id rather than by list position for the same reason.

  lir-jumps-to-header : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-jumps-to-header (insns) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiJump (b) -> if b == 0 then True else lir-jumps-to-header insns (i + 1) n
    is otherwise -> lir-jumps-to-header insns (i + 1) n

  lir-has-backedge-blocks : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-has-backedge-blocks (blocks) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in if lir-jumps-to-header (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) then True
      else lir-has-backedge-blocks blocks (i + 1) n

  lir-has-backedge : LirFunc -> Boolean
  lir-has-backedge (f) =
   lir-has-backedge-blocks (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks))

Section: Lowering

 Lowering threads an environment mapping names to the operands they were
 bound to (down the tree, extended at each let), and returns the operand a
 subtree produced together with the running vreg counter, the completed
 blocks, the current block being built (its id and accumulating
 instructions), the next free block id, and a flag that is False the
 moment any construct outside the whitelist is met. A False anywhere
 propagates to the top and the whole function is declared not lowerable —
 this increment never lowers a partial function.

  LirEnvEntry = record {
   name : Text,
   val : LirValue
  }

 A self-tail-recursive function is a loop, and the loop context is what
 lets the lowering see it as one. `fname` and `np` identify the function
 being lowered; `header` is the block a back-edge jumps to -- block 0, the
 entry, where the parameters already occupy vregs `0 .. np-1`. `tail` is
 the one field that changes as the walk descends: it is True only where the
 value being lowered *is* the function's result, so an `IrApply` there
 never returns to its caller and can become a jump instead of a call.
 Operands, conditions, arguments, scrutinees and let-values are never in
 tail position; `if`/`match` arms and a `let` body inherit whatever the
 enclosing expression had.

  LirLoopCtx = record {
   active : Boolean,
   tail : Boolean,
   fname : Text,
   np : Integer,
   header : Integer
  }

  loop-notail : LirLoopCtx -> LirLoopCtx
  loop-notail (lc) =
   if lc.tail == False then lc
   else LirLoopCtx { active = lc.active, tail = False, fname = lc.fname, np = lc.np, header = lc.header }

 A back-edge fires only for a tail call to this very function with exactly
 its parameter count. Anything else -- a call to another function, or a
 self-call that is not in tail position (`fib` and `fact` call themselves
 as operands) -- stays an ordinary `LiCall`, so the DAG path is untouched.

  lir-backedge-fires : LirLoopCtx, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-backedge-fires (lc) (root) (nargs) =
   if lc.active == False then False
   else if lc.tail == False then False
   else if root /= lc.fname then False
   else nargs == lc.np

 `term` records that a path ended in a terminator of its own -- today only
 a back-edge. A terminated path produces no value, and no join move or jump
 may be appended after its `LiJump`, or the block would carry two
 terminators and everything after the first would be unreachable.

  LowerRes = record {
   val : LirValue,
   counter : Integer,
   blocks : List LirBlock,
   cur : Integer,
   curi : List LirInsn,
   bctr : Integer,
   ok : Boolean,
   term : Boolean
  }

  lir-env-find : List LirEnvEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-env-find (env) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at env i).name == n then i
   else lir-env-find env n (i + 1) len

 The whitelisted value operators, and the fallthrough that rejects every
 other IR binary op (comparisons, boolean, division, remainder, append,
 vector) in value position by leaving the function un-lowered.

  lir-op-of : IRBinaryOp -> LirOp
  lir-op-of (op) =
   when op
    is IrSubInt -> LoSub
    is IrMulInt -> LoMul
    is IrRemInt -> LoRem
    is IrDivInt -> LoDiv
    is otherwise -> LoAdd

  lir-op-whitelisted : IRBinaryOp -> Boolean
  lir-op-whitelisted (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddInt | IrSubInt | IrMulInt | IrRemInt | IrDivInt -> True
    is otherwise -> False

 Dividing or taking a remainder by a positive power of two is a shift or a
 mask, not a division: it fixes no registers, clobbers nothing, and is one
 instruction where `idiv` is four. The tree emitter has always done this, which
 is why `collatz` -- `math-mod n 2 == 0` and `n / 2` -- compiles to `test` and
 `sar` there. Bringing division into the LIR without bringing this too would
 have made collatz worse, not better.

 It matters here beyond the instruction count. A power-of-two division is not
 a division as far as the register file is concerned, so it must not be
 recorded as one: marking it would mask RAX and RDX out of every value live
 across it for a clobber that never happens.

  lir-imm-pow2 : LirValue -> Boolean
  lir-imm-pow2 (v) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> if k > 0 then bit-and k (k - 1) == 0 else False
    is LvReg (r) -> False

  lir-op-divides : LirOp -> Boolean
  lir-op-divides (op) =
   when op
    is LoRem -> True
    is LoDiv -> True
    is otherwise -> False

 The comparison operators a fused condition accepts, and the mapping to a
 branch condition. Any other operator in condition position falls to the
 value path (compare the materialised boolean against zero).

  lir-cmp-whitelisted : IRBinaryOp -> Boolean
  lir-cmp-whitelisted (op) =
   when op
    is IrEq | IrNotEq | IrLt | IrGt | IrLtEq | IrGtEq -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  lir-cond-of : IRBinaryOp -> LirCond
  lir-cond-of (op) =
   when op
    is IrEq -> LcEq
    is IrNotEq -> LcNe
    is IrLt -> LcLt
    is IrGt -> LcGt
    is IrLtEq -> LcLe
    is IrGtEq -> LcGe
    is otherwise -> LcNe

  lower-fail : Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer -> LowerRes
  lower-fail (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) =
   LowerRes { val = LvImm 0, counter = counter, blocks = blocks, cur = cur, curi = curi, bctr = bctr, ok = False, term = False }

 Closing an `if` or `match` arm. An arm that already terminated -- it ended in
 a back-edge -- is left exactly as it is: it has no value to move and its
 `LiJump` is already the block's terminator.

 An arm in tail position returns. Its value is the function's value, so there
 is nothing to carry to a join and nothing after the join to run: `LiRet`
 leaves from where the value was computed. An arm that is not in tail position
 has somewhere to go afterwards, so it moves into the shared result vreg and
 jumps to the join, which is what gives that vreg a definition on every path.

 The shared vreg is what this avoids, and it was never cheap. Every arm
 defines it, so its single interval runs from the first arm to the exit and
 spans everything between -- including every call -- which forces it into a
 callee-saved register and costs a push, a pop, and a move to RAX at the end,
 to carry a value each arm already had.

  lir-close-arm : List LirInsn, LirValue, Integer, Integer, Boolean, Boolean -> List LirInsn
  lir-close-arm (curi) (v) (d) (join-id) (term) (tail) =
   if term then curi
   else if tail then list-push curi (deck-record (LiRet v))
   else list-push (list-push curi (deck-record (LiMove d v))) (deck-record (LiJump join-id))

  lower-lir-expr : List LirEnvEntry, IRExpr, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> LowerRes
  lower-lir-expr (env) (e) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (depth) (lc) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) -> LowerRes { val = LvImm v, counter = counter, blocks = blocks, cur = cur, curi = curi, bctr = bctr, ok = True, term = False }
    is IrName (n) (t) (s) ->
     let i = lir-env-find env n 0 (list-length env)
     in if i < 0 then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
        else LowerRes { val = (list-at env i).val, counter = counter, blocks = blocks, cur = cur, curi = curi, bctr = bctr, ok = True, term = False }
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) ->
     if lir-op-whitelisted op == False then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
     else let rl = lower-lir-expr env l counter blocks cur curi bctr (depth + 1) (loop-notail lc)
     in if rl.ok == False then rl
        else let rr = lower-lir-expr env r (rl.counter) (rl.blocks) (rl.cur) (rl.curi) (rl.bctr) (depth + 1) (loop-notail lc)
        in if rr.ok == False then rr
           else let dst = rr.counter
           in LowerRes {
            val = LvReg dst,
            counter = dst + 1,
            blocks = rr.blocks,
            cur = rr.cur,
            curi = list-push (rr.curi) (deck-record (LiBin (lir-op-of op) dst (rl.val) (rr.val))),
            bctr = rr.bctr,
            ok = True,
            term = False
           }
    is IrLet (n) (t) (v) (b) (s) ->
     let rv = lower-lir-expr env v counter blocks cur curi bctr (depth + 1) (loop-notail lc)
     in if rv.ok == False then rv
        else lower-lir-expr ((deck-record (LirEnvEntry { name = n, val = rv.val })) :: env) b (rv.counter) (rv.blocks) (rv.cur) (rv.curi) (rv.bctr) (depth + 1) lc
    is IrIf (c) (th) (el) (t) (s) ->
     let cres = lower-if-cond env c counter blocks cur curi bctr (depth + 1) (loop-notail lc)
     in if cres.ok == False then lower-fail (cres.counter) (cres.blocks) (cres.cur) (cres.curi) (cres.bctr)
        else let d = cres.counter
        in let then-id = cres.bctr
        in let else-id = cres.bctr + 1
        in let join-id = cres.bctr + 2
        in let curi-term = list-push (cres.curi) (deck-record (LiBranch (cres.cond) then-id else-id))
        in let blocks1 = list-push (cres.blocks) (deck-record (LirBlock { id = cres.cur, insns = curi-term }))
        in let rt = lower-lir-expr env th (d + 1) blocks1 then-id [] (cres.bctr + 3) (depth + 1) lc
        in if rt.ok == False then rt
           else let rt-insns = lir-close-arm (rt.curi) (rt.val) d join-id (rt.term) (lc.tail)
           in let blocks2 = list-push (rt.blocks) (deck-record (LirBlock { id = rt.cur, insns = rt-insns }))
           in let re = lower-lir-expr env el (rt.counter) blocks2 else-id [] (rt.bctr) (depth + 1) lc
           in if re.ok == False then re
              else let re-insns = lir-close-arm (re.curi) (re.val) d join-id (re.term) (lc.tail)
              in let blocks3 = list-push (re.blocks) (deck-record (LirBlock { id = re.cur, insns = re-insns }))
              in LowerRes {
               val = LvReg d,
               counter = re.counter,
               blocks = blocks3,
               cur = join-id,
               curi = [],
               bctr = re.bctr,
               ok = True,
               term = if lc.tail then True else (rt.term) & (re.term)
              }
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (mt) (ms) ->
     let nb = list-length branches
     in if nb == 0 then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
        else if match-branches-lowerable branches 0 nb == False then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
        else let rs = lower-lir-expr env scrut counter blocks cur curi bctr (depth + 1) (loop-notail lc)
        in if rs.ok == False then rs
           else lower-match-chain env branches 0 nb (rs.val) (rs.counter) (rs.bctr) (rs.counter + 1) (rs.blocks) (rs.cur) (rs.curi) (rs.bctr + 1) (depth + 1) lc
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (s) ->
     let root = lir-apply-root-name e
     in if root == "" then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
        else if lir-env-find env root 0 (list-length env) >= 0 then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
        else let args = lir-apply-args e []
        in if list-length args > 6 then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
           else if lir-backedge-fires lc root (list-length args)
                then lower-backedge env args 0 (list-length args) counter blocks cur curi bctr [] (depth + 1) lc
                else lower-call-args env args 0 (list-length args) root counter blocks cur curi bctr [] (depth + 1) (loop-notail lc)
    is otherwise -> lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr

 Lowering a call. A curried apply flattens to a root name and a left-to-
 right argument list; a root that is not a plain name (an indirect call
 through a lambda or field) leaves `root` empty and declines. A root that IS
 a plain name but names a locally bound value -- a parameter or let-binding
 that shadows a global, i.e. a closure being applied -- also declines: the
 call must go indirect through that value, and `LiCall` only names a global
 target. Emitting the name regardless would call the global the local
 shadows (`apply2 (gsub) x y = gsub x y` with a `gsub` parameter would call
 the global `gsub`, not the passed closure). The tree emitter checks
 `lookup-local` first for exactly this reason; the selector declines to it.
 Each argument is lowered to an operand in order, threading the block state,
 and the call becomes one `LiCall` whose destination is a fresh vreg -- the
 result. Only the structural shape is decided here: whether the named target
 is actually a plain callable user function (not a constructor, builtin, or
 effect op) is the selector's gate, where the arity and builtin tables live. Constructor
 patterns still bind through `LiLoad`; a constructor *application* (which
 allocates) is declined by that selector gate, not here.

  lir-apply-root-name : IRExpr -> Text
  lir-apply-root-name (e) =
   when e
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (s) -> lir-apply-root-name f
    is IrName (n) (t) (s) -> n
    is otherwise -> ""

  lir-apply-args : IRExpr, List IRExpr -> List IRExpr
  lir-apply-args (e) (acc) =
   when e
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (s) -> lir-apply-args f ([a] & acc)
    is otherwise -> acc

  lower-call-args : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, List LirValue, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> LowerRes
  lower-call-args (env) (args) (i) (na) (name) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (argvals) (depth) (lc) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
   else if i >= na
    then let dst = counter
     in LowerRes { val = LvReg dst, counter = dst + 1, blocks = blocks, cur = cur, curi = list-push curi (deck-record (LiCall dst name argvals)), bctr = bctr, ok = True, term = False }
    else let ra = lower-lir-expr env (list-at args i) counter blocks cur curi bctr depth lc
    in if ra.ok == False then ra
       else lower-call-args env args (i + 1) na name (ra.counter) (ra.blocks) (ra.cur) (ra.curi) (ra.bctr) (list-push argvals (ra.val)) depth lc

 Lowering a self-tail-call as a back-edge. The arguments are lowered first,
 as ordinary non-tail values, exactly as a call would lower them; what
 differs is where they land. Instead of a `LiCall`, each argument is moved
 into the parameter vreg it becomes on the next iteration and control jumps
 to the header.

 Those moves are a parallel assignment, not a sequence, and writing them
 naively corrupts the loop: `f b a` would move `b` into `p0` and then read
 the already-overwritten `p0` for `p1`. The fix is to read every parameter
 before writing any. An argument whose value is a bare parameter vreg is
 staged into a fresh temp first; every other argument is already in a fresh
 vreg that no parameter write can disturb. Then the parameter writes happen
 together, reading only temps and non-parameter vregs. This costs one extra
 move per parameter-valued argument and nothing at all for a loop like
 `sum-to (n - 1) (acc + n)`, whose arguments are both computed values.

  StageRes = record {
   counter : Integer,
   curi : List LirInsn,
   staged : List LirValue
  }

  lir-stage-args : List LirValue, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirInsn, List LirValue -> StageRes
  lir-stage-args (argvals) (i) (na) (np) (counter) (curi) (staged) =
   if i >= na then StageRes { counter = counter, curi = curi, staged = staged }
   else let v = list-at argvals i
   in when v
    is LvReg (r) ->
     if r < np
      then let t = counter
       in lir-stage-args argvals (i + 1) na np (t + 1) (list-push curi (deck-record (LiMove t v))) (list-push staged (LvReg t))
      else lir-stage-args argvals (i + 1) na np counter curi (list-push staged v)
    is LvImm (k) -> lir-stage-args argvals (i + 1) na np counter curi (list-push staged v)

  lir-write-params : List LirValue, Integer, Integer, List LirInsn -> List LirInsn
  lir-write-params (staged) (i) (na) (curi) =
   if i >= na then curi
   else lir-write-params staged (i + 1) na (list-push curi (deck-record (LiMove i (list-at staged i))))

 Staging every parameter-valued argument is correct but pays moves the loop
 rarely needs, and the reason is the order. `sum-to (n - 1) (acc + n)` emits
 `n - 1` first, so the write of `p0` is fenced from its own definition by
 `acc + n`, which reads `p0` in between -- and coalescing, correctly, refuses.
 Emit `acc + n` FIRST and both writes fold away to `add rsi, rdi; sub rdi, 1`.

 So order the arguments before lowering: the argument that writes `p_i` goes
 after every argument that reads `p_i`. That is a topological sort, and its
 cycles are real -- `f b a` needs `p0` written after `p1` is read and `p1`
 after `p0` is read. A swap has no order, which is precisely what the staging
 temps are for, so a cycle falls back to the path below rather than inventing
 one.

 The writes follow the order too, and must: with `f 5 a` the order is `[1, 0]`,
 and writing `p0 <- 5` before `p1 <- p0` would hand the next iteration the
 constant instead of the old parameter.

 Reordering is sound because these arguments are pure. The lowering takes
 literals, names, arithmetic, `let`, `if`, `match` and `apply` and declines
 everything else, so no act block, effect operation or handler can occur
 inside one -- and Codex keeps effects in the type, so a body that lowers at
 all is not calling anything effectful. `lir-arg-reads` is conservative by
 construction: a shape it does not model reads every parameter, which forces
 the fallback rather than an unsound reorder.

  lir-arg-reads : List LirEnvEntry, IRExpr, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-arg-reads (env) (e) (p) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then True
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) -> False
    is IrBoolLit (v) (s) -> False
    is IrName (n) (t) (s) ->
     let i = lir-env-find env n 0 (list-length env)
     in if i < 0 then True
        else when (list-at env i).val
         is LvReg (r) -> r == p
         is LvImm (k) -> False
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) ->
     if lir-arg-reads env l p (depth + 1) then True
     else lir-arg-reads env r p (depth + 1)
    is otherwise -> True

  lir-ord-blocked : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, List Boolean, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-ord-blocked (env) (args) (i) (na) (done) (j) =
   if j >= na then False
   else if j == i then lir-ord-blocked env args i na done (j + 1)
   else if list-at done j then lir-ord-blocked env args i na done (j + 1)
   else if lir-arg-reads env (list-at args j) i 0 then True
   else lir-ord-blocked env args i na done (j + 1)

  lir-ord-pick : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> Integer
  lir-ord-pick (env) (args) (i) (na) (done) =
   if i >= na then 0 - 1
   else if list-at done i then lir-ord-pick env args (i + 1) na done
   else if lir-ord-blocked env args i na done 0 then lir-ord-pick env args (i + 1) na done
   else i

  lir-ord-loop : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, List Boolean, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-ord-loop (env) (args) (k) (na) (done) (acc) =
   if k >= na then acc
   else let p = lir-ord-pick env args 0 na done
   in if p < 0 then []
      else lir-ord-loop env args (k + 1) na (list-set-at done p True) (list-push acc p)

  lir-order-args : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-order-args (env) (args) (na) =
   if na <= 0 then []
   else lir-ord-loop env args 0 na (lir-make-vec 0 na []) []

  lir-vals-init : Integer, Integer, List LirValue -> List LirValue
  lir-vals-init (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-vals-init (i + 1) n (list-push acc (LvImm 0))

  lir-write-ord : List LirValue, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirInsn -> List LirInsn
  lir-write-ord (vals) (order) (k) (na) (curi) =
   if k >= na then curi
   else let ix = list-at order k
   in lir-write-ord vals order (k + 1) na (list-push curi (deck-record (LiMove ix (list-at vals ix))))

  lower-backedge-ord : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, List LirValue, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> LowerRes
  lower-backedge-ord (env) (args) (order) (k) (na) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (vals) (depth) (lc) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
   else if k >= na
    then LowerRes {
     val = LvImm 0,
     counter = counter,
     blocks = blocks,
     cur = cur,
     curi = list-push (lir-write-ord vals order 0 na curi) (deck-record (LiJump (lc.header))),
     bctr = bctr,
     ok = True,
     term = True
    }
    else let ix = list-at order k
    in let ra = lower-lir-expr env (list-at args ix) counter blocks cur curi bctr depth (loop-notail lc)
    in if ra.ok == False then ra
       else lower-backedge-ord env args order (k + 1) na (ra.counter) (ra.blocks) (ra.cur) (ra.curi) (ra.bctr) (list-set-at vals ix (ra.val)) depth lc

  lower-backedge : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, List LirValue, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> LowerRes
  lower-backedge (env) (args) (i) (na) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (argvals) (depth) (lc) =
   let order = lir-order-args env args na
   in if list-length order == na
      then lower-backedge-ord env args order 0 na counter blocks cur curi bctr (lir-vals-init 0 na []) depth lc
      else lower-backedge-stage env args i na counter blocks cur curi bctr argvals depth lc

  lower-backedge-stage : List LirEnvEntry, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, List LirValue, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> LowerRes
  lower-backedge-stage (env) (args) (i) (na) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (argvals) (depth) (lc) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
   else if i >= na
    then let sr = lir-stage-args argvals 0 na (lc.np) counter curi []
     in let with-writes = lir-write-params (sr.staged) 0 na (sr.curi)
     in LowerRes {
      val = LvImm 0,
      counter = sr.counter,
      blocks = blocks,
      cur = cur,
      curi = list-push with-writes (deck-record (LiJump (lc.header))),
      bctr = bctr,
      ok = True,
      term = True
     }
    else let ra = lower-lir-expr env (list-at args i) counter blocks cur curi bctr depth (loop-notail lc)
    in if ra.ok == False then ra
       else lower-backedge-stage env args (i + 1) na (ra.counter) (ra.blocks) (ra.cur) (ra.curi) (ra.bctr) (list-push argvals (ra.val)) depth lc

 Lowering an `if` condition. A comparison lowers its two operands as
 values and emits a `LiCmp`, carrying the branch condition up; any other
 boolean lowers as a value operand compared against zero (non-zero is
 true). Either way the compare lands in the current block, and the caller
 terminates that block with the branch.

 One shape is worth recognising before that: a remainder by a power of two
 compared against zero. `math-mod n 2 == 0` is how a program asks whether a
 number is even, and it would otherwise lower as a move, an `and` (the
 power-of-two peephole already turns the division into that much) and a
 compare, where one `test` sets the same flag. The tree emitter has always
 written the single instruction -- `is-rem-pow2-pattern` in the x86 chapter
 is the same rule -- and the recognition here is deliberately its twin.
 Copying it is the point: two emitters that disagree about what a program
 means are a worse problem than one that is slow, so the guard is the same
 guard and the shape is the same shape.

 The soundness argument is not the obvious one, and it is the reason this is
 restricted to a comparison against zero rather than that being the case
 that happened to be needed. `and` and remainder do **not** agree in
 general: for a negative dividend they differ in sign and in value, because
 the remainder follows the quotient's rounding and the mask does not. What
 they agree on -- for every dividend, of either sign -- is whether the
 result is zero, because that asks only whether `2^k` divides the number,
 and divisibility does not care which way the quotient rounded. This fusion
 asks that one question and no other.

  CondRes = record {
   cond : LirCond,
   counter : Integer,
   blocks : List LirBlock,
   cur : Integer,
   curi : List LirInsn,
   bctr : Integer,
   ok : Boolean
  }

  cond-fail : Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer -> CondRes
  cond-fail (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) =
   CondRes { cond = LcNe, counter = counter, blocks = blocks, cur = cur, curi = curi, bctr = bctr, ok = False }

 The mask a power-of-two remainder tests against, or minus one for any
 expression that is not one. `dv - 1` is the mask exactly when `dv` is a
 positive power of two, which is what `bit-and dv (dv - 1) == 0` says.

  lir-rem-pow2-mask : IRExpr -> Integer
  lir-rem-pow2-mask (expr) =
   when expr
    is IrBinary (op) (base) (divisor) (ty) (sp) ->
     when op
      is IrRemInt ->
       when divisor
        is IrIntLit (dv) (ds) -> if dv > 0 & bit-and dv (dv - 1) == 0 then dv - 1 else (0 - 1)
        is otherwise -> (0 - 1)
      is otherwise -> (0 - 1)
    is otherwise -> (0 - 1)

  lir-rem-base : IRExpr -> IRExpr
  lir-rem-base (expr) =
   when expr
    is IrBinary (op) (base) (divisor) (ty) (sp) -> base
    is otherwise -> expr

 Only equality against zero. `test` sets ZF from the AND and the branch reads
 it; an ordering comparison would be reading a sign the mask never
 established, and for a negative dividend the mask and the remainder do not
 even agree in sign. Equality is the question the two forms answer alike.

  lir-cond-is-zero-test : IRBinaryOp, IRExpr -> Boolean
  lir-cond-is-zero-test (op) (r) =
   when r
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) ->
     if v /= 0 then False
     else when op
      is IrEq -> True
      is IrNotEq -> True
      is otherwise -> False
    is otherwise -> False

  lower-if-cond : List LirEnvEntry, IRExpr, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> CondRes
  lower-if-cond (env) (c) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (depth) (lc) =
   when c
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) ->
     if lir-cond-is-zero-test op r & lir-rem-pow2-mask l >= 0
      then lower-if-test env op l counter blocks cur curi bctr depth lc
     else if lir-cmp-whitelisted op == False then cond-value-path env c counter blocks cur curi bctr depth lc
     else let rl = lower-lir-expr env l counter blocks cur curi bctr (depth + 1) lc
     in if rl.ok == False then cond-fail (rl.counter) (rl.blocks) (rl.cur) (rl.curi) (rl.bctr)
        else let rr = lower-lir-expr env r (rl.counter) (rl.blocks) (rl.cur) (rl.curi) (rl.bctr) (depth + 1) lc
        in if rr.ok == False then cond-fail (rr.counter) (rr.blocks) (rr.cur) (rr.curi) (rr.bctr)
           else CondRes {
            cond = lir-cond-of op,
            counter = rr.counter,
            blocks = rr.blocks,
            cur = rr.cur,
            curi = list-push (rr.curi) (deck-record (LiCmp (rl.val) (rr.val))),
            bctr = rr.bctr,
            ok = True
           }
    is otherwise -> cond-value-path env c counter blocks cur curi bctr depth lc

 The fused form: lower the dividend alone and test it against the mask. The
 remainder is never computed and the comparison never emitted -- `test` sets
 ZF from the AND it does not keep, which is exactly the flag the branch is
 about to read.

  lower-if-test : List LirEnvEntry, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> CondRes
  lower-if-test (env) (op) (l) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (depth) (lc) =
   let rb = lower-lir-expr env (lir-rem-base l) counter blocks cur curi bctr (depth + 1) lc
   in if rb.ok == False then cond-fail (rb.counter) (rb.blocks) (rb.cur) (rb.curi) (rb.bctr)
      else CondRes {
       cond = lir-cond-of op,
       counter = rb.counter,
       blocks = rb.blocks,
       cur = rb.cur,
       curi = list-push (rb.curi) (deck-record (LiTest (rb.val) (LvImm (lir-rem-pow2-mask l)))),
       bctr = rb.bctr,
       ok = True
      }

  cond-value-path : List LirEnvEntry, IRExpr, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> CondRes
  cond-value-path (env) (c) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (depth) (lc) =
   let rv = lower-lir-expr env c counter blocks cur curi bctr (depth + 1) lc
   in if rv.ok == False then cond-fail (rv.counter) (rv.blocks) (rv.cur) (rv.curi) (rv.bctr)
      else CondRes {
       cond = LcNe,
       counter = rv.counter,
       blocks = rv.blocks,
       cur = rv.cur,
       curi = list-push (rv.curi) (deck-record (LiCmp (rv.val) (LvImm 0))),
       bctr = rv.bctr,
       ok = True
      }

 Lowering `match`. This increment covers the scalar-switch subset: a
 scrutinee that lowers as a value (integer/name/arithmetic/`if`) matched by
 literal, variable, and wildcard patterns, with no guard. An or-pattern
 (`is 0 | 1 -> body`) needs no special handling: the parser already expands
 it into one branch per alternative, each carrying the same body, so each
 alternative becomes its own literal test jumping to a copy of the body --
 correct, if not yet deduplicated. The whole match is declined --
 `lower-def-to-lir` keeps the tree form -- if any branch has a constructor
 or vector pattern (they need a tag/field load a later increment adds) or a
 real guard (anything but `IrBoolLit True`), or if the scrutinee itself is
 not value-lowerable.

  guard-trivial : IRExpr -> Boolean
  guard-trivial (g) =
   when g
    is IrBoolLit (v) (s) -> v
    is otherwise -> False

 The constructor layout, target-independent and derived from the `SumTy`
 exactly as the tree emitter derives it. A variant value is a pointer to
 `[tag : 8 bytes][fields]`; the tag is the constructor's declaration index,
 and fields pack widest-first (an 8-byte-field constructor lays out at
 `8 + i*8`). These reimplement `find-ctor-tag` / `ctor-field-types` /
 `field-byte-width` / `field-byte-signed` / `sum-ctor-field-offset` from
 the x86 emitter over the shared width table (`bounds-to-hw-width`,
 `hw-width-bytes`, `hw-width-signed` in the Types quire), so the LIR stays
 free of any target-specific chapter and a plug can reuse it. The width
 table itself is the one load-bearing part, and it is shared, not copied.

  lir-ctor-tag : List SumCtor, Text, Integer -> Integer
  lir-ctor-tag (ctors) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ctors then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at ctors i).name.value == name then i
   else lir-ctor-tag ctors name (i + 1)

  lir-ctor-fields-find : List SumCtor, Text, Integer -> List CodexType
  lir-ctor-fields-find (ctors) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ctors then []
   else if (list-at ctors i).name.value == name then (list-at ctors i).fields
   else lir-ctor-fields-find ctors name (i + 1)

  lir-field-width : CodexType -> Integer
  lir-field-width (ty) =
   when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (mode) -> hw-width-bytes (bounds-to-hw-width lo hi)
    is BooleanTy -> 1
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> lir-field-width inner
    is LinearTy (linner) -> lir-field-width linner
    is otherwise -> 8

  lir-field-signed : CodexType -> Boolean
  lir-field-signed (ty) =
   when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (mode) -> hw-width-signed (bounds-to-hw-width lo hi)
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> lir-field-signed inner
    is LinearTy (linner) -> lir-field-signed linner
    is otherwise -> False

  lir-fields-before : List CodexType, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-fields-before (tys) (target-w) (target-i) (j) (acc) =
   if j >= list-length tys then acc
   else let jw = lir-field-width (list-at tys j)
   in if jw > target-w then lir-fields-before tys target-w target-i (j + 1) (acc + jw)
      else if jw == target-w & j < target-i then lir-fields-before tys target-w target-i (j + 1) (acc + jw)
      else lir-fields-before tys target-w target-i (j + 1) acc

  lir-field-offset : List CodexType, Integer -> Integer
  lir-field-offset (tys) (i) =
   8 + lir-fields-before tys (lir-field-width (list-at tys i)) i 0 0

 A constructor pattern is lowerable when its scrutinee type resolved to a
 `SumTy`, the named constructor has a tag, and every sub-pattern is a
 variable or a wildcard. A nested constructor, literal, or vector
 sub-pattern needs recursion this increment does not do, so it declines.

  ctor-subs-simple : List IRPat, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ctor-subs-simple (subs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else when (list-at subs i)
    is IrVarPat (nm) (t) (s) -> ctor-subs-simple subs (i + 1) n
    is IrWildPat (s) -> ctor-subs-simple subs (i + 1) n
    is otherwise -> False

  pat-lowerable : IRPat -> Boolean
  pat-lowerable (p) =
   when p
    is IrWildPat (s) -> True
    is IrVarPat (nm) (t) (s) -> True
    is IrLitPat (v) (t) (s) -> True
    is IrCtorPat (nm) (subs) (t) (s) ->
     when t
      is SumTy (sn) (sa) (cs) -> if lir-ctor-tag cs nm 0 >= 0 then ctor-subs-simple subs 0 (list-length subs) else False
      is otherwise -> False
    is otherwise -> False

  pat-always-matches : IRPat -> Boolean
  pat-always-matches (p) =
   when p
    is IrWildPat (s) -> True
    is IrVarPat (nm) (t) (s) -> True
    is otherwise -> False

 Binding a pattern into the current block. A variable binds the scrutinee
 operand directly; a wildcard or literal binds nothing; a constructor
 emits one `LiLoad` per variable field at its packed offset and width,
 binding the field name to the loaded vreg. The record threads the running
 vreg counter and the block's instruction list.

  PatBindRes = record {
   env : List LirEnvEntry,
   counter : Integer,
   curi : List LirInsn
  }

  lir-bind-ctor-loads : List LirEnvEntry, List IRPat, List CodexType, LirValue, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirInsn -> PatBindRes
  lir-bind-ctor-loads (env) (subs) (tys) (sv) (i) (n) (counter) (curi) =
   if i >= n then PatBindRes { env = env, counter = counter, curi = curi }
   else when (list-at subs i)
    is IrVarPat (nm) (t) (s) ->
     let w = if i < list-length tys then lir-field-width (list-at tys i) else 8
     in let sg = if i < list-length tys then lir-field-signed (list-at tys i) else False
     in let off = if i < list-length tys then lir-field-offset tys i else (1 + i) * 8
     in let dst = counter
     in lir-bind-ctor-loads ((deck-record (LirEnvEntry { name = nm, val = LvReg dst })) :: env) subs tys sv (i + 1) n (counter + 1) (list-push curi (deck-record (LiLoad dst sv off w sg)))
    is otherwise -> lir-bind-ctor-loads env subs tys sv (i + 1) n counter curi

  lir-bind-pattern : List LirEnvEntry, IRPat, LirValue, Integer, List LirInsn -> PatBindRes
  lir-bind-pattern (env) (p) (sv) (counter) (curi) =
   when p
    is IrVarPat (nm) (t) (s) -> PatBindRes { env = (deck-record (LirEnvEntry { name = nm, val = sv })) :: env, counter = counter, curi = curi }
    is IrCtorPat (nm) (subs) (t) (s) ->
     let tys = when t is SumTy (sn) (sa) (cs) -> lir-ctor-fields-find cs nm 0 is otherwise -> []
     in lir-bind-ctor-loads env subs tys sv 0 (list-length subs) counter curi
    is otherwise -> PatBindRes { env = env, counter = counter, curi = curi }

 Emitting a branch's discriminating test into the current block. A literal
 arm compares the scrutinee against the literal; a constructor arm loads
 the tag into a fresh vreg and compares it against the constructor's tag.
 Both are followed by an equality branch (the caller adds the `LiBranch`),
 so the test record carries the extended instruction list and the advanced
 vreg counter (a constructor test consumes one vreg for the tag).

  MatchTestRes = record {
   curi : List LirInsn,
   counter : Integer
  }

  lir-match-test : IRPat, LirValue, Integer, List LirInsn -> MatchTestRes
  lir-match-test (p) (sv) (counter) (curi) =
   when p
    is IrCtorPat (nm) (subs) (t) (s) ->
     let tagval = when t is SumTy (sn) (sa) (cs) -> lir-ctor-tag cs nm 0 is otherwise -> 0 - 1
     in let tag-dst = counter
     in MatchTestRes { curi = list-push (list-push curi (deck-record (LiLoad tag-dst sv 0 8 False))) (deck-record (LiCmp (LvReg tag-dst) (LvImm tagval))), counter = counter + 1 }
    is IrLitPat (v) (t) (s) -> MatchTestRes { curi = list-push curi (deck-record (LiCmp sv (LvImm (lit-text-to-integer v)))), counter = counter }
    is otherwise -> MatchTestRes { curi = list-push curi (deck-record (LiCmp sv (LvImm 0))), counter = counter }

  match-branch-ok : IRBranch -> Boolean
  match-branch-ok (b) =
   if pat-lowerable (b.pattern) then guard-trivial (b.guard) else False

  match-branches-lowerable : List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  match-branches-lowerable (bs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else if match-branch-ok (list-at bs i) then match-branches-lowerable bs (i + 1) n else False

 The decision chain. A branch that always matches -- a wildcard or variable
 pattern, or the last branch, which an exhaustive match reaches only when
 it must match -- lowers its body unconditionally into the current block,
 first binding the pattern (a variable to the scrutinee, a constructor's
 fields with loads). A tested branch that is not the catch-all emits its
 discriminating test (a literal compare, or a tag load and compare) ending
 the current block in an equality branch to its body block or the next test
 block, then binds the pattern and lowers the body in the body block. Every
 arm's body moves its value into the shared result vreg `d` and jumps to
 the join, so the verifier sees `d` defined on every path into the join.
 The literal value comes from `lit-text-to-integer` and the tag from the
 constructor's declaration index, exactly as the tree emitter's
 `emit-pattern` derives them, so the two agree.

  lower-match-chain : List LirEnvEntry, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer, LirValue, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, LirLoopCtx -> LowerRes
  lower-match-chain (env) (branches) (i) (n) (sv) (d) (join-id) (counter) (blocks) (cur) (curi) (bctr) (depth) (lc) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then lower-fail counter blocks cur curi bctr
   else let b = list-at branches i
   in let p = b.pattern
   in if (pat-always-matches p) | (i >= n - 1)
      then let bind = lir-bind-pattern env p sv counter curi
           in let rb = lower-lir-expr (bind.env) (b.body) (bind.counter) blocks cur (bind.curi) bctr (depth + 1) lc
           in if rb.ok == False then rb
              else let body-insns = lir-close-arm (rb.curi) (rb.val) d join-id (rb.term) (lc.tail)
              in let blocks2 = list-push (rb.blocks) (deck-record (LirBlock { id = rb.cur, insns = body-insns }))
              in LowerRes { val = LvReg d, counter = rb.counter, blocks = blocks2, cur = join-id, curi = [], bctr = rb.bctr, ok = True, term = lc.tail }
      else let tst = lir-match-test p sv counter curi
           in let body-id = bctr
           in let next-id = bctr + 1
           in let cur-term = list-push (tst.curi) (deck-record (LiBranch LcEq body-id next-id))
           in let blocks1 = list-push blocks (deck-record (LirBlock { id = cur, insns = cur-term }))
           in let bind = lir-bind-pattern env p sv (tst.counter) []
           in let rb = lower-lir-expr (bind.env) (b.body) (bind.counter) blocks1 body-id (bind.curi) (bctr + 2) (depth + 1) lc
           in if rb.ok == False then rb
              else let body-insns = lir-close-arm (rb.curi) (rb.val) d join-id (rb.term) (lc.tail)
              in let blocks2 = list-push (rb.blocks) (deck-record (LirBlock { id = rb.cur, insns = body-insns }))
              in lower-match-chain env branches (i + 1) n sv d join-id (rb.counter) blocks2 next-id [] (rb.bctr) (depth + 1) lc

 Seed the environment with one entry per parameter, in declaration order,
 bound to vregs 0, 1, 2, ...; the vreg counter starts past them, block 0
 is the entry, and the next free block id starts at 1. A function whose
 body lowers cleanly has its final current block sealed as the exit block
 and becomes a `LirFunc`; otherwise the result's `ok` is False and the
 caller keeps the tree form.

  LowerFuncRes = record {
   func : LirFunc,
   ok : Boolean
  }

  lir-seed-params : List IRParam, Integer, Integer, List LirEnvEntry -> List LirEnvEntry
  lir-seed-params (ps) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else lir-seed-params ps (i + 1) len (list-push acc (deck-record (LirEnvEntry { name = (list-at ps i).name, val = LvReg i })))

  lir-empty-func : Text -> LirFunc
  lir-empty-func (nm) = LirFunc { name = nm, nparams = 0, blocks = [ deck-record (LirBlock { id = 0, insns = [] }) ], result = LvImm 0, nvregs = 0, rets = False }

 The loop context starts active and in tail position: the body's value is
 the function's result. Block 0 is the header -- the entry block, where the
 parameters are already in vregs `0 .. np-1`, which is exactly the state a
 back-edge must restore.

 A body that terminates on every path has already returned everything it is
 going to, through a `LiRet` in each arm or a back-edge. There is no result
 operand and no exit block to fall into, and `rets` records that so the
 selector emits no trailing epilogue. A body that does not -- a bare
 expression, whose value simply *is* the result -- keeps the old shape and
 the final block is its exit.

 The last block is pushed either way, empty when every path returned. It is
 unreachable then, and it costs nothing to emit, but it keeps the block ids
 dense, which every table indexed by id depends on.

  lower-def-to-lir : IRDef -> LowerFuncRes
  lower-def-to-lir (d) =
   let np = list-length (d.params)
   in let env0 = lir-seed-params (d.params) 0 np []
   in let lc0 = LirLoopCtx { active = True, tail = True, fname = d.name, np = np, header = 0 }
   in let r = lower-lir-expr env0 (d.body) np [] 0 [] 1 0 lc0
   in if r.ok == False then LowerFuncRes { func = lir-empty-func (d.name), ok = False }
      else let blocks-final = list-push (r.blocks) (deck-record (LirBlock { id = r.cur, insns = r.curi }))
      in let f0 = LirFunc {
       name = d.name,
       nparams = np,
       blocks = blocks-final,
       result = if r.term then LvImm 0 else r.val,
       nvregs = r.counter,
       rets = r.term
      }
      in LowerFuncRes {
       func = lir-coalesce-func f0 lir-coalesce-fuel,
       ok = True
      }

Section: Move Coalescing

 A destination-driven `if`/`match` writes each arm's value into a shared
 result vreg with a `LiMove`, and nested joins stack those moves
 (`ve -> d-inner`, `d-inner -> d-outer`) -- each becomes a real register
 move and each forces the allocator to keep another callee-saved register
 live across the arm. This pass folds them away before allocation. A move
 `LiMove d (LvReg s)` whose source `s` is a non-parameter used nowhere but
 that move is a pure copy: rewrite every definition of `s` to define `d`
 directly, and drop the move. `s` then has no definition and no use, so the
 interval builder gives it a degenerate range that expires before the first
 real interval and it costs no register. Iterated to a fixed point the
 whole join chain collapses onto the outermost result vreg. It is a
 structural, deterministic rewrite -- the byte-identical fixed point is
 preserved -- and the allocation verifier still validates the result.

  lir-coalesce-fuel : Integer
  lir-coalesce-fuel = 4096

  CoalCand = record {
   found : Boolean,
   d : Integer,
   s : Integer
  }

  lcoal-srcreg : LirValue -> Integer
  lcoal-srcreg (v) =
   when v
    is LvReg (r) -> r
    is LvImm (k) -> (0 - 1)

 Count operand uses per vreg across the function; definitions do not count.

  lcoal-cu-val : LirValue, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lcoal-cu-val (v) (uc) (nv) =
   when v
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < 0 then uc else if r >= nv then uc else list-set-at uc r ((list-at uc r) + 1)
    is LvImm (k) -> uc

  lcoal-cu-list : List LirValue, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lcoal-cu-list (args) (uc) (nv) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then uc
   else lcoal-cu-list args (lcoal-cu-val (list-at args i) uc nv) nv (i + 1) n

  lcoal-cu-insn : LirInsn, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lcoal-cu-insn (ins) (uc) (nv) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> lcoal-cu-val r (lcoal-cu-val l uc nv) nv
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> lcoal-cu-val src uc nv
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> lcoal-cu-val base uc nv
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> lcoal-cu-val r (lcoal-cu-val l uc nv) nv
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> lcoal-cu-val r (lcoal-cu-val l uc nv) nv
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> lcoal-cu-list args uc nv 0 (list-length args)
    is LiRet (v) -> lcoal-cu-val v uc nv
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> uc
    is LiJump (b) -> uc

  lcoal-cu-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lcoal-cu-insns (insns) (i) (len) (uc) (nv) =
   if i >= len then uc
   else lcoal-cu-insns insns (i + 1) len (lcoal-cu-insn (list-at insns i) uc nv) nv

  lcoal-cu-blocks : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lcoal-cu-blocks (blocks) (i) (len) (uc) (nv) =
   if i >= len then uc
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in lcoal-cu-blocks blocks (i + 1) len (lcoal-cu-insns (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) uc nv) nv

 The function result is a use. When the function does not return from every
 path, the epilogue reads the result operand, and a vreg that is both the
 result and the source of exactly one join move is used twice, not once.
 Counting only instruction operands here let coalescing delete the result's
 definition: the join move looked like the sole use, the definition was
 rewritten to define the join vreg, and the result was left reading a vreg
 nothing defines -- which the default assignment placed in RAX, so the
 function answered with whatever the last arm happened to leave there.

  lcoal-count-uses : LirFunc -> List Integer
  lcoal-count-uses (f) =
   let uc = lcoal-cu-blocks (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) (lir-int-vec 0 (f.nvregs) 0 []) (f.nvregs)
   in if f.rets then uc else lcoal-cu-val (f.result) uc (f.nvregs)

 Find the first coalescable move: `LiMove d (LvReg s)` with `s` a
 non-parameter, distinct from `d`, and used exactly once.

 Coalescing rewrites `s`'s definition to define `d` directly, which hoists
 that definition up to wherever `s` was computed. Two things must hold for the
 hoist to be sound: nothing between the two reads `d` (or the read sees the new
 value early), AND the hoist must not push a definition of `d` onto a path
 where `d` is otherwise defined (or `d` ends up defined twice on one path).
 Both are guaranteed when `s` is defined in the SAME block as the move and
 nothing between reads `d` -- which is exactly `lcoal-param-safe`, and it now
 gates EVERY destination, parameter or join.

 A back-edge makes `d` a parameter, and the read condition is real. `sum-to
 (n - 1) (acc + n)` lowers to `v = p0 - 1`, `w = p1 + p0`, `p0 <- v`, `p1 <- w`.
 Coalescing `p1 <- w` is safe: between `w`'s definition and the move, nothing
 reads `p1`. Coalescing `p0 <- v` is not: `w = p1 + p0` sits in between and
 reads `p0`, so folding the subtract into `p0` would make the loop add the
 decremented `n` and compute the wrong sum.

 A join vreg `d` is fresh and unread, so the read condition is vacuous -- but
 the second condition is NOT, and treating join destinations as always-safe
 (the old `dst >= np` bypass) was wrong. A simple join move `d <- w` has `w`
 computed in the same arm block, so the same-block check passes and the fold
 proceeds as before. A join-into-join -- `d2 <- d1` where `d1` is itself an
 earlier if's result, defined in that if's arm blocks -- has `d1` defined in a
 different block, so `lcoal-defs-at` returns -1 and the candidate is refused.
 Folding it anyway hoists `d2`'s definition up into the first if's arms, which
 lie on the same path as a later `d2 <- 0`, leaving `d2` defined twice on one
 path: a legal overwrite the emitter handles but the structural verifier
 rightly rejects (CDX9007). Refusing the fold keeps the one `mov` and the LIR
 stays single-def-per-path.

 So the check is the actual condition: find `s`'s definition, require it be in
 this block, and require that no instruction strictly between it and the move
 reads `d`. A candidate whose definition is in another block is refused rather
 than reasoned about. Reads of `d` after the move are not a hazard -- the new
 value is precisely what the next iteration must see.

  lcoal-reads : LirInsn, Integer -> Boolean
  lcoal-reads (ins) (d) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> ((lcoal-srcreg l) == d) | ((lcoal-srcreg r) == d)
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> (lcoal-srcreg src) == d
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> (lcoal-srcreg base) == d
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> ((lcoal-srcreg l) == d) | ((lcoal-srcreg r) == d)
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> ((lcoal-srcreg l) == d) | ((lcoal-srcreg r) == d)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> lcoal-args-read args d 0 (list-length args)
    is LiRet (v) -> (lcoal-srcreg v) == d
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> False
    is LiJump (b) -> False

  lcoal-args-read : List LirValue, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lcoal-args-read (args) (d) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else if (lcoal-srcreg (list-at args i)) == d then True
   else lcoal-args-read args d (i + 1) n

  lcoal-defs-at : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lcoal-defs-at (insns) (i) (len) (s) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (lcoal-dst (list-at insns i)) == s then i
   else lcoal-defs-at insns (i + 1) len s

  lcoal-none-reads : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lcoal-none-reads (insns) (i) (stop) (d) =
   if i >= stop then True
   else if lcoal-reads (list-at insns i) d then False
   else lcoal-none-reads insns (i + 1) stop d

  lcoal-param-safe : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lcoal-param-safe (insns) (len) (m) (d) (s) =
   let df = lcoal-defs-at insns 0 len s
   in if df < 0 then False
      else if df > m then False
      else lcoal-none-reads insns (df + 1) m d

  lcoal-find-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> CoalCand
  lcoal-find-insns (insns) (i) (len) (uc) (np) =
   if i >= len then CoalCand { found = False, d = 0, s = 0 }
   else let ins = list-at insns i
   in when ins
    is LiMove (dst) (src) ->
     let r = lcoal-srcreg src
     in if (r >= np) & (r >= 0) & (r /= dst) & (r < list-length uc) & ((list-at uc r) == 1)
             & (lcoal-param-safe insns len i dst r)
        then CoalCand { found = True, d = dst, s = r }
        else lcoal-find-insns insns (i + 1) len uc np
    is otherwise -> lcoal-find-insns insns (i + 1) len uc np

  lcoal-find-blocks : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> CoalCand
  lcoal-find-blocks (blocks) (i) (len) (uc) (np) =
   if i >= len then CoalCand { found = False, d = 0, s = 0 }
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let c = lcoal-find-insns (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) uc np
   in if c.found then c else lcoal-find-blocks blocks (i + 1) len uc np

 Apply a coalesce (d, s): drop the unique `LiMove _ (LvReg s)` and rewrite
 every instruction defining `s` to define `d` instead.

  lcoal-dst : LirInsn -> Integer
  lcoal-dst (ins) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> dst
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> dst
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> dst
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> dst
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> (0 - 1)
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> (0 - 1)
    is LiRet (v) -> (0 - 1)
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> (0 - 1)
    is LiJump (b) -> (0 - 1)

  lcoal-set-dst : LirInsn, Integer -> LirInsn
  lcoal-set-dst (ins) (nd) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> deck-record (LiBin op nd l r)
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> deck-record (LiMove nd src)
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> deck-record (LiLoad nd base off w sg)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> deck-record (LiCall nd name args)
    is otherwise -> ins

  lcoal-is-move-from : LirInsn, Integer -> Boolean
  lcoal-is-move-from (ins) (s) =
   when ins
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> (lcoal-srcreg src) == s
    is otherwise -> False

  lcoal-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirInsn -> List LirInsn
  lcoal-insns (insns) (i) (len) (d) (s) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let ins = list-at insns i
   in if lcoal-is-move-from ins s then lcoal-insns insns (i + 1) len d s acc
      else if (lcoal-dst ins) == s then lcoal-insns insns (i + 1) len d s (list-push acc (lcoal-set-dst ins d))
      else lcoal-insns insns (i + 1) len d s (list-push acc ins)

  lcoal-blocks : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirBlock -> List LirBlock
  lcoal-blocks (blocks) (i) (len) (d) (s) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let ni = lcoal-insns (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) d s []
   in lcoal-blocks blocks (i + 1) len d s (list-push acc (deck-record (LirBlock { id = b.id, insns = ni })))

  lir-coalesce-func : LirFunc, Integer -> LirFunc
  lir-coalesce-func (f) (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then f
   else let uc = lcoal-count-uses f
   in let c = lcoal-find-blocks (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) uc (f.nparams)
   in if c.found == False then f
      else let nb = lcoal-blocks (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) (c.d) (c.s) []
      in lir-coalesce-func (LirFunc { name = f.name, nparams = f.nparams, blocks = nb, result = f.result, nvregs = f.nvregs, rets = f.rets }) (fuel - 1)

Section: Structural Verifier

 The destination-driven invariant the design doc (LIR.md section 4) states
 for the allocator to rely on, generalised from a straight line to a DAG
 of blocks: every vreg is defined exactly once, and every use reads a vreg
 that is defined on every path reaching it. A violation here is a lowering
 bug — the LIR is malformed before any allocation happens — and it is
 reported, not tolerated, so a bad lowering is caught at the instruction
 that made it rather than three stages downstream. The allocation verifier
 (a map from physical location to vreg-set) is a separate, later checker;
 this one needs no allocation to run.

 Define-once has exactly one exception, and it arrived with back-edges: a
 loop assigns its parameters again on every iteration. So in a function with
 a back-edge a *parameter* may be defined a second time, and nothing else may
 be. `redef-limit` below carries that permission, and it is zero for every
 function that has no back-edge, which is how the rule stays exactly as
 strict as it was for the DAG the LIR mostly still is.

 The walk processes blocks in list order, which is topological, so every
 predecessor of a block is processed before it. Each block is entered with
 a definedness set — the pointwise intersection of the exit sets of its
 predecessors, because a vreg is defined on entry only if it is defined on
 every incoming edge. That intersection at the join is what catches a use
 of a vreg that only one arm defined. Definedness is a flat boolean vector
 indexed by vreg id, sized to `nvregs`; a fresh copy is walked per block so
 that in-place mutation never corrupts a stored entry set.

  lir-make-vec : Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-make-vec (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-make-vec (i + 1) n (list-push acc False)

  lir-mark-params : List Boolean, Integer, Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-mark-params (defined) (i) (np) =
   if i >= np then defined
   else lir-mark-params (list-set-at defined i True) (i + 1) np

  lir-vec-copy : List Boolean, Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-vec-copy (src) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-vec-copy src (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at src i))

  lir-vec-intersect : List Boolean, List Boolean, Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-vec-intersect (a) (b) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-vec-intersect a b (i + 1) n (list-push acc (if list-at a i then list-at b i else False))

  lir-init-entries : Integer, Integer, List (List Boolean) -> List (List Boolean)
  lir-init-entries (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-init-entries (i + 1) n (list-push acc [])

  lir-val-defined : LirValue, List Boolean, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-val-defined (v) (defined) (nvregs) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> True
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < 0 then False else if r >= nvregs then False else list-at defined r

 `redef-limit` is how many leading vregs -- the parameters -- may be defined
 a second time. It is zero for a function without a back-edge, which is the
 define-once rule exactly as it stood. For a loop it is the parameter count:
 a back-edge assigns the next iteration's parameters, so each parameter is
 defined once at entry and once per back-edge, and that is the loop, not a
 malformed lowering. The exemption stops at the parameters, so a second
 definition of any computed vreg is still reported, and it is withheld
 entirely from functions that have no back-edge to justify it.

  lir-def-msg : Text, Integer, List Boolean, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  lir-def-msg (fname) (dst) (defined) (nvregs) (redef-limit) (msgs) =
   if dst < 0 then list-push msgs ((fname) & ": negative dst vreg")
   else if dst >= nvregs then list-push msgs ((fname) & ": dst vreg out of range")
   else if (list-at defined dst) & (dst >= redef-limit) then list-push msgs ((fname) & ": vreg " & integer-to-text dst & " defined twice")
   else msgs

  lir-def-set : Integer, List Boolean, Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-def-set (dst) (defined) (nvregs) =
   if dst < 0 then defined
   else if dst >= nvregs then defined
   else list-set-at defined dst True

  LirWalk = record {
   defined : List Boolean,
   msgs : List Text
  }

  lir-use-msg : Text, Integer, Integer, LirValue, List Boolean, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  lir-use-msg (fname) (bid) (i) (v) (defined) (nvregs) (msgs) =
   if lir-val-defined v defined nvregs then msgs
   else list-push msgs ((fname) & ": use before def in block " & integer-to-text bid & " insn " & integer-to-text i)

  lir-use-msg-list : Text, Integer, Integer, List LirValue, List Boolean, Integer, List Text, Integer, Integer -> List Text
  lir-use-msg-list (fname) (bid) (ii) (args) (defined) (nvregs) (msgs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then msgs
   else lir-use-msg-list fname bid ii args defined nvregs (lir-use-msg fname bid ii (list-at args i) defined nvregs msgs) (i + 1) n

  lir-check-block-insns : Text, Integer, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Boolean, List Text, Integer -> LirWalk
  lir-check-block-insns (fname) (bid) (nvregs) (insns) (i) (len) (defined) (msgs) (rl) =
   if i >= len then LirWalk { defined = defined, msgs = msgs }
   else let ins = list-at insns i
   in when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) ->
     let m1 = lir-use-msg fname bid i l defined nvregs msgs
     in let m2 = lir-use-msg fname bid i r defined nvregs m1
     in let m3 = lir-def-msg fname dst defined nvregs rl m2
     in lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len (lir-def-set dst defined nvregs) m3 rl
    is LiMove (dst) (src) ->
     let m1 = lir-use-msg fname bid i src defined nvregs msgs
     in let m3 = lir-def-msg fname dst defined nvregs rl m1
     in lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len (lir-def-set dst defined nvregs) m3 rl
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) ->
     let m1 = lir-use-msg fname bid i base defined nvregs msgs
     in let m3 = lir-def-msg fname dst defined nvregs rl m1
     in lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len (lir-def-set dst defined nvregs) m3 rl
    is LiCmp (l) (r) ->
     let m1 = lir-use-msg fname bid i l defined nvregs msgs
     in let m2 = lir-use-msg fname bid i r defined nvregs m1
     in lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len defined m2 rl
    is LiTest (l) (r) ->
     let m1 = lir-use-msg fname bid i l defined nvregs msgs
     in let m2 = lir-use-msg fname bid i r defined nvregs m1
     in lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len defined m2 rl
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) ->
     let m1 = lir-use-msg-list fname bid i args defined nvregs msgs 0 (list-length args)
     in let m2 = lir-def-msg fname dst defined nvregs rl m1
     in lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len (lir-def-set dst defined nvregs) m2 rl
    is LiRet (v) ->
     let m1 = lir-use-msg fname bid i v defined nvregs msgs
     in lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len defined m1 rl
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len defined msgs rl
    is LiJump (b) -> lir-check-block-insns fname bid nvregs insns (i + 1) len defined msgs rl

 The successors of a block are named by its terminator, which is its last
 instruction. A block without a terminator (the exit block) has no
 successors and its exit set is where the function result must be defined.

  lir-block-succs : List LirInsn -> List Integer
  lir-block-succs (insns) =
   let n = list-length insns
   in if n == 0 then []
      else let last = list-at insns (n - 1)
      in when last
       is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> list-push (list-push [] bt) be
       is LiJump (b) -> list-push [] b
       is otherwise -> []

  PropRes = record {
   entries : List (List Boolean),
   seen : List Boolean
  }

  lir-propagate : List (List Boolean), List Boolean, List Boolean, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> PropRes
  lir-propagate (entries) (seen) (exit) (succs) (i) (len) (nvregs) =
   if i >= len then PropRes { entries = entries, seen = seen }
   else let sid = list-at succs i
   in if (list-at seen sid) == False
      then let e2 = list-set-at entries sid (lir-vec-copy exit 0 nvregs [])
           in lir-propagate e2 (list-set-at seen sid True) exit succs (i + 1) len nvregs
      else let inter = lir-vec-intersect (list-at entries sid) exit 0 nvregs []
           in lir-propagate (list-set-at entries sid inter) seen exit succs (i + 1) len nvregs

  LirCheckState = record {
   msgs : List Text,
   last-defined : List Boolean
  }

  lir-check-blocks : Text, Integer, List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List (List Boolean), List Boolean, List Text, List Boolean, Integer -> LirCheckState
  lir-check-blocks (fname) (nvregs) (blocks) (i) (len) (entries) (seen) (msgs) (last-defined) (rl) =
   if i >= len then LirCheckState { msgs = msgs, last-defined = last-defined }
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let running = lir-vec-copy (list-at entries (b.id)) 0 nvregs []
   in let w = lir-check-block-insns fname (b.id) nvregs (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) running msgs rl
   in let succs = lir-block-succs (b.insns)
   in let prop = lir-propagate entries seen (w.defined) succs 0 (list-length succs) nvregs
   in lir-check-blocks fname nvregs blocks (i + 1) len (prop.entries) (prop.seen) (w.msgs) (w.defined) rl

 A back-edge propagates into the header, which the walk has already passed,
 so the header's entry set is intersected after the fact and never re-walked
 -- the walk is one topological pass, not a fixpoint. That is sound here and
 not by luck: the header's entry set is the parameters, a back-edge's exit
 set contains the parameters (it just assigned them), and the intersection of
 the two is the parameters again. The set cannot shrink, so there is nothing
 a second pass would discover. A LIR whose loops carried something else would
 break that argument, which is the same condition the selector's gate refuses.

  lir-check-func : LirFunc -> List Text
  lir-check-func (f) =
   let nb = list-length (f.blocks)
   in let nv = f.nvregs
   in let entries0 = lir-init-entries 0 nb []
   in let pv = lir-mark-params (lir-make-vec 0 nv []) 0 (f.nparams)
   in let entries1 = list-set-at entries0 0 pv
   in let seen1 = list-set-at (lir-make-vec 0 nb []) 0 True
   in let rl = if lir-has-backedge f then f.nparams else 0
   in let cs = lir-check-blocks (f.name) nv (f.blocks) 0 nb entries1 seen1 [] (lir-make-vec 0 nv []) rl
   in if lir-val-defined (f.result) (cs.last-defined) nv then (cs.msgs)
      else list-push (cs.msgs) ((f.name) & ": result operand uses an undefined vreg")

Section: Liveness

 Backward liveness over the block DAG, the foundation the linear-scan
 allocator needs (LIR.md section 5: "Liveness is one backward pass over
 blocks in reverse topological order"). Because the LIR has no loops --
 flattening a loop-free tree yields a DAG -- there is no fixpoint: a single
 backward sweep suffices. A vreg is live at a point if some path from there
 uses it before redefining it.

 The transfer over one instruction, applied to the live-after set to get
 live-before: kill the defined vreg first, then add the used operands (so a
 self-referential `v = op v ...` would keep v live, though the
 destination-driven lowering never emits one). A comparison and the
 terminators define nothing.

  lir-vec-union : List Boolean, List Boolean, Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-vec-union (a) (b) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-vec-union a b (i + 1) n (list-push acc (if list-at a i then True else list-at b i))

  lir-live-kill : List Boolean, Integer, Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-live-kill (live) (dst) (nv) =
   if dst < 0 then live else if dst >= nv then live else list-set-at live dst False

  lir-live-use : LirValue, List Boolean, Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-live-use (v) (live) (nv) =
   when v
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < 0 then live else if r >= nv then live else list-set-at live r True
    is LvImm (k) -> live

  lir-live-use-list : List LirValue, List Boolean, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-live-use-list (args) (live) (nv) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then live
   else lir-live-use-list args (lir-live-use (list-at args i) live nv) nv (i + 1) n

  lir-insn-live-before : LirInsn, List Boolean, Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-insn-live-before (ins) (live) (nv) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> lir-live-use l (lir-live-use r (lir-live-kill live dst nv) nv) nv
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> lir-live-use src (lir-live-kill live dst nv) nv
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> lir-live-use base (lir-live-kill live dst nv) nv
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> lir-live-use l (lir-live-use r live nv) nv
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> lir-live-use l (lir-live-use r live nv) nv
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> lir-live-use-list args (lir-live-kill live dst nv) nv 0 (list-length args)
    is LiRet (v) -> lir-live-use v live nv
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> live
    is LiJump (b) -> live

  lir-insns-live-in : List LirInsn, Integer, List Boolean, Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-insns-live-in (insns) (i) (live) (nv) =
   if i < 0 then live
   else lir-insns-live-in insns (i - 1) (lir-insn-live-before (list-at insns i) live nv) nv

 A block's live-out is the union of its successors' live-in. The single
 exit block (no successors) instead starts from the function result: the
 return value is live out of the function, so the allocator must keep it in
 a register to the end. Live-in per block is stored by block id (ids are
 dense) and the sweep runs blocks in reverse list order -- the list is
 topological, so a block's successors are always later in the list and have
 already been computed when the sweep reaches it. No fixpoint, no revisit.

  lir-seed-result : LirValue, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-seed-result (res) (nv) (vec) =
   when res
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < 0 then vec else if r >= nv then vec else list-set-at vec r True
    is LvImm (k) -> vec

  lir-succ-union : List (List Boolean), List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-succ-union (livein) (succs) (i) (n) (nv) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-succ-union livein succs (i + 1) n nv (lir-vec-union acc (list-at livein (list-at succs i)) 0 nv [])

  lir-init-empty-vecs : Integer, Integer, Integer, List (List Boolean) -> List (List Boolean)
  lir-init-empty-vecs (i) (nb) (nv) (acc) =
   if i >= nb then acc
   else lir-init-empty-vecs (i + 1) nb nv (list-push acc (lir-make-vec 0 nv []))

  lir-liveness-loop : List LirBlock, LirValue, Integer, List (List Boolean), Integer -> List (List Boolean)
  lir-liveness-loop (blocks) (result) (i) (livein) (nv) =
   if i < 0 then livein
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let succs = lir-block-succs (b.insns)
   in let liveout = if list-length succs == 0 then lir-seed-result result nv (lir-make-vec 0 nv []) else lir-succ-union livein succs 0 (list-length succs) nv (lir-make-vec 0 nv [])
   in let bin = lir-insns-live-in (b.insns) (list-length (b.insns) - 1) liveout nv
   in lir-liveness-loop blocks result (i - 1) (list-set-at livein (b.id) bin) nv

 A back-edge points backwards, so one reverse sweep is no longer enough: the
 block carrying the jump is visited before the header it jumps to, and reads
 the header's live-in while it is still the initial empty vector. The sweep
 is therefore iterated until nothing changes. Liveness is monotone -- a sweep
 only ever adds live vregs -- so the iteration terminates, and for a single
 loop it settles in two sweeps; the fuel is a backstop, not the mechanism.

 The comparison needs care. `list-set-at` writes in place and hands back the
 same list, so the sweep mutates `livein` rather than producing a new one,
 and comparing the sweep's result against the list handed to it would compare
 a list with itself and report "no change" on the first pass every time. What
 is captured first is a fresh outer list holding the previous per-block
 vectors; the sweep replaces outer slots with newly built vectors and never
 writes into a vector already stored, so the captured pointers still read the
 old contents.

  lir-vec-eq : List Boolean, List Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-vec-eq (a) (b) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then True
   else if (list-at a i) /= (list-at b i) then False
   else lir-vec-eq a b (i + 1) n

  lir-livein-eq : List (List Boolean), List (List Boolean), Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-livein-eq (a) (b) (i) (nb) (nv) =
   if i >= nb then True
   else if lir-vec-eq (list-at a i) (list-at b i) 0 nv == False then False
   else lir-livein-eq a b (i + 1) nb nv

  lir-snapshot-vecs : List (List Boolean), Integer, Integer, List (List Boolean) -> List (List Boolean)
  lir-snapshot-vecs (v) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-snapshot-vecs v (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at v i))

  lir-liveness-fix : List LirBlock, LirValue, List (List Boolean), Integer, Integer, Integer -> List (List Boolean)
  lir-liveness-fix (blocks) (result) (livein) (nv) (nb) (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then livein
   else let prev = lir-snapshot-vecs livein 0 nb []
   in let next = lir-liveness-loop blocks result (nb - 1) livein nv
   in if lir-livein-eq prev next 0 nb nv then next
      else lir-liveness-fix blocks result next nv nb (fuel - 1)

 A function with no back-edge keeps the exact single-sweep behaviour it had:
 the list order is topological, one sweep is already the fixpoint, and the
 iteration is skipped rather than paying a second confirming sweep on every
 function the compiler emits.

  lir-liveness : LirFunc -> List (List Boolean)
  lir-liveness (f) =
   let nb = list-length (f.blocks)
   in let nv = f.nvregs
   in let livein0 = lir-init-empty-vecs 0 nb nv []
   in if lir-has-backedge f
      then lir-liveness-fix (f.blocks) (f.result) livein0 nv nb (nb + 2)
      else lir-liveness-loop (f.blocks) (f.result) (nb - 1) livein0 nv

Section: Allocator

 The linear-scan register allocator (LIR.md section 5: Wimmer 2005 minus
 splitting, on a DAG). It replaces the trivial bijective placeholder with a
 real allocation: values whose live ranges do not overlap share a register,
 and when registers run out a whole interval is spilled to a stack slot,
 chosen by furthest-next-use (Belady). Its output is the same `List
 Location` assignment the allocation verifier (next section) checks, so
 every allocation is validated on the spot -- the checker stood before the
 allocator by design.

 The loop-free property of the LIR (the tree IR has no loops, so a
 flattened body is a DAG) buys the simplifications: an interval is a single
 [start, fin] range over a flat instruction numbering, not a range-list
 with holes. That is a conservative interval -- it can only over-state
 liveness (keep a value in a register or slot longer than needed), never
 under-state it, so it is safe, and the allocation verifier backstops any
 lowering that would break the assumption. Interval splitting,
 rematerialisation, and stack-slot recycling are v2 (LIR.md section 9);
 this increment is whole-interval spill with one dedicated slot per spilled
 vreg.

 The register file is a flat count here -- `lir-alloc-nregs` allocatable
 registers, numbered 0.. -- of which the first `lir-alloc-ncallee` are the
 callee-saved "local" registers the x86 emitter fills first (RBX, R12, R13,
 R14; `docs/ArchitectsSketchbook.md`) and the remainder are caller-saved
 registers a call may clobber. That distinction is the one fixed-interval
 constraint the design names (LIR.md section 5) that bites: a caller-saved
 register cannot hold a value live across a call. So an interval that crosses
 a call is restricted to the callee-saved pool (or a spill slot); an interval
 that does not may take a caller-saved one.

 The caller-saved part is split again, and that split is what a leaf turns on.
 Index `lir-alloc-ncallee` is RAX, which any function may use for a value that
 crosses nothing. `[lir-alloc-nargregs, lir-alloc-nregs)` are the argument
 registers, and only a function that makes **no call** may touch them: in
 anything else they are needed to place arguments, and a call would clobber
 them anyway. A leaf has neither problem, so it takes them first -- which is
 how it comes to push nothing (the selector saves only indices below
 `lir-alloc-ncallee`, and a leaf uses none) and how its parameters land in the
 registers they arrived in (the argument registers are listed in argument
 order and the parameters are scanned first, in order, against a free file).

 The physical mapping (abstract index -> x86 register) still lives in the
 selector; the pool structure lives here, so the register file stays data --
 a different target is a different count and a different set of splits.

 Determinism is a correctness requirement (LIR.md section 6): the fixed
 point is byte-identity, so every choice is fully ordered. Intervals are
 processed by start then vreg; the chosen free register is the lowest in its
 pool; the spill victim is the active interval of greatest fin, ties broken by
 greatest vreg.

  lir-alloc-ncallee : Integer
  lir-alloc-ncallee = 4

  lir-alloc-nargregs : Integer
  lir-alloc-nargregs = 5

  lir-alloc-nregs : Integer
  lir-alloc-nregs = 9

 The two registers a division fixes. A call clobbers every caller-saved
 register and a value live across one has to retreat to the callee-saved pool;
 a division clobbers exactly two, and treating it like a call would be far too
 blunt -- `gcd`'s parameters are carried around its loop and therefore live
 across its remainder, so the callee-saved pool would cost it two pushes and
 two pops to avoid two registers it never wanted.

 So they are named. On x86-64 `idiv` takes the dividend in RDX:RAX and leaves
 the remainder in RDX, which is abstract index 4 (RAX) and index 7 (RDX, the
 third argument register). Like the rest of the pool structure this is data: a
 target that fixes different registers names different indices, and one that
 fixes none has nothing to exclude.

  lir-alloc-diva : Integer
  lir-alloc-diva = 4

  lir-alloc-divb : Integer
  lir-alloc-divb = 7

 An interval is a vreg's live range over a flat numbering of the function's
 instructions (blocks in list order, which is topological; instructions
 numbered 0..). `start` is the position of the vreg's first definition --
 `-1` for a parameter, defined before the first instruction -- and `fin` is
 the position of its last use, extended to one past the last instruction
 for the function result (which is live out of the function). A vreg never
 used takes `fin = start`, a zero-length interval.

  LirIvl = record {
   vreg : Integer,
   start : Integer,
   fin : Integer
  }

  lir-int-vec : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-int-vec (i) (n) (fill) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-int-vec (i + 1) n fill (list-push acc fill)

 A use at position `p` extends the operand's `fin` to `p` (max); a def at
 `p` sets the vreg's `start` to `p` on its first occurrence (defs are met in
 increasing position order, so the first is the minimum). Immediates carry
 no interval.

  lir-iv-use : LirValue, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-iv-use (v) (fin) (p) (nv) =
   when v
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < 0 then fin else if r >= nv then fin else if p > (list-at fin r) then list-set-at fin r p else fin
    is LvImm (k) -> fin

  lir-iv-def : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-iv-def (d) (start) (p) (nv) =
   if d < 0 then start else if d >= nv then start else if (list-at start d) == (0 - 2) then list-set-at start d p else start

  LirIvAcc = record {
   start : List Integer,
   fin : List Integer,
   p : Integer
  }

  lir-iv-use-list : List LirValue, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-iv-use-list (args) (fin) (p) (nv) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then fin
   else lir-iv-use-list args (lir-iv-use (list-at args i) fin p nv) p nv (i + 1) n

 An instruction occupies two positions, not one: it reads at `p` and writes
 at `p + 1`. The single-slot numbering could not tell a read from the write
 that happens beside it, so an operand's last use and the definition born
 there landed on the same position, expiry (`fin < start`) kept the operand
 alive, and the definition was pushed onto a different register. That is the
 "read-then-overwrite the abstract LIR does not model yet" the expiry rule
 refers to. It is modelled now.

 What it buys is exactly what the tree emitter does and this could not: a
 call's result may live in the register its argument just vacated -- RAX --
 instead of being moved out of RAX into a callee-saved register and back.
 The arguments are read into the argument registers before the call executes
 and the result is written after, so `arg.fin = p` and `dst.start = p + 1` is
 the truth, and the register is genuinely free.

 It is safe for every instruction here except one. `LiMove` writes its
 destination from its source, so sharing means `mov w, w`, which elides.
 `LiLoad` computes its address before it writes, so `mov w, [w + off]` is
 fine. `LiCall` reads its arguments into the argument registers first. Only
 `LiBin` is a hazard, and only on its right operand: the two-operand lowering
 is `mov w, l; op w, r`, which writes `w` before it reads `r`, so a
 destination sharing the right operand's register would read a value it had
 already destroyed. `lir-sel-bin` handles that case rather than the numbering
 pretending it cannot arise.

  lir-iv-insn : LirInsn, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> LirIvAcc
  lir-iv-insn (ins) (start) (fin) (p) (nv) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) ->
     let f1 = lir-iv-use l fin p nv
     in let f2 = lir-iv-use r f1 p nv
     in LirIvAcc { start = lir-iv-def dst start (p + 1) nv, fin = f2, p = p + 2 }
    is LiMove (dst) (src) ->
     let f1 = lir-iv-use src fin p nv
     in LirIvAcc { start = lir-iv-def dst start (p + 1) nv, fin = f1, p = p + 2 }
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) ->
     let f1 = lir-iv-use base fin p nv
     in LirIvAcc { start = lir-iv-def dst start (p + 1) nv, fin = f1, p = p + 2 }
    is LiCmp (l) (r) ->
     let f1 = lir-iv-use l fin p nv
     in let f2 = lir-iv-use r f1 p nv
     in LirIvAcc { start = start, fin = f2, p = p + 2 }
    is LiTest (l) (r) ->
     let f1 = lir-iv-use l fin p nv
     in let f2 = lir-iv-use r f1 p nv
     in LirIvAcc { start = start, fin = f2, p = p + 2 }
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) ->
     let f1 = lir-iv-use-list args fin p nv 0 (list-length args)
     in LirIvAcc { start = lir-iv-def dst start (p + 1) nv, fin = f1, p = p + 2 }
    is LiRet (v) -> LirIvAcc { start = start, fin = lir-iv-use v fin p nv, p = p + 2 }
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> LirIvAcc { start = start, fin = fin, p = p + 2 }
    is LiJump (b) -> LirIvAcc { start = start, fin = fin, p = p + 2 }

  lir-iv-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> LirIvAcc
  lir-iv-insns (insns) (i) (len) (start) (fin) (p) (nv) =
   if i >= len then LirIvAcc { start = start, fin = fin, p = p }
   else let a = lir-iv-insn (list-at insns i) start fin p nv
   in lir-iv-insns insns (i + 1) len (a.start) (a.fin) (a.p) nv

  lir-iv-blocks : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> LirIvAcc
  lir-iv-blocks (blocks) (i) (len) (start) (fin) (p) (nv) =
   if i >= len then LirIvAcc { start = start, fin = fin, p = p }
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let a = lir-iv-insns (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) start fin p nv
   in lir-iv-blocks blocks (i + 1) len (a.start) (a.fin) (a.p) nv

 Parameters are defined before the first instruction, so their `start` is
 `-1`. A vreg whose `fin` was never set by a use (still the `-2` sentinel)
 takes `fin = start` -- a dead definition still occupies its register for
 the instant it is defined. The function result's live range extends to one
 past the last instruction, so the allocator keeps it in place to the exit.

  lir-iv-params : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-iv-params (start) (i) (np) =
   if i >= np then start
   else lir-iv-params (list-set-at start i (0 - 1)) (i + 1) np

  lir-iv-fin-fix : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-iv-fin-fix (start) (fin) (i) (nv) =
   if i >= nv then fin
   else if (list-at fin i) == (0 - 2) then lir-iv-fin-fix start (list-set-at fin i (list-at start i)) (i + 1) nv
   else lir-iv-fin-fix start fin (i + 1) nv

  lir-iv-result : LirValue, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-iv-result (res) (fin) (nins) (nv) =
   when res
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < 0 then fin else if r >= nv then fin else if nins > (list-at fin r) then list-set-at fin r nins else fin
    is LvImm (k) -> fin

 A vreg that is never defined gets no interval and no register. Coalescing
 leaves these behind: folding `p1 <- w` rewrites `w`'s defining instruction to
 define `p1`, and `w` is then mentioned nowhere -- no definition, no use -- yet
 it still owns an id below `nvregs`. Its `start` is still the `-2` sentinel,
 because `lir-iv-params` only rewrites the parameters (to `-1`) and
 `lir-iv-fin-fix` only touches `fin`.

 Left in, they are not harmless. Every one of them starts at `-2`, and expiry
 is `fin < start`, so `-2 < -2` is false and they do not expire against each
 other: they stack up and take a register apiece. They all expire before the
 first parameter at `-1`, so no live value is affected and this was invisible
 in the output -- but the assignments stay behind, and `lir-nused-callee`
 reads the whole assignment table. In `ack` five dead vregs claimed r4, r0, r1,
 r2 and r3, and r3 is R14, so the prologue pushed and the epilogue popped a
 callee-saved register the body never mentions.

 A vreg that is defined and never used is a different thing and keeps its
 interval: the instruction defining it is still emitted and still needs
 somewhere to write.

  lir-iv-assemble : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirIvl -> List LirIvl
  lir-iv-assemble (start) (fin) (i) (nv) (acc) =
   if i >= nv then acc
   else if (list-at start i) == (0 - 2)
        then lir-iv-assemble start fin (i + 1) nv acc
        else lir-iv-assemble start fin (i + 1) nv (list-push acc (deck-record (LirIvl { vreg = i, start = list-at start i, fin = list-at fin i })))

 The intervals are sorted by start ascending, ties preserved in vreg order
 (a stable insertion sort: an interval is inserted before the first one of
 strictly greater start). Because `lir-iv-assemble` builds them in vreg
 order, equal starts stay ordered by vreg, which is the determinism the
 fixed point needs.

  lir-iv-append-rest : List LirIvl, List LirIvl, Integer, Integer -> List LirIvl
  lir-iv-append-rest (acc) (src) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-iv-append-rest (list-push acc (list-at src i)) src (i + 1) n

  lir-iv-insert : LirIvl, List LirIvl, Integer, Integer, List LirIvl -> List LirIvl
  lir-iv-insert (x) (sorted) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then list-push acc x
   else let e = list-at sorted i
   in if (e.start) > (x.start) then lir-iv-append-rest (list-push acc x) sorted i n
      else lir-iv-insert x sorted (i + 1) n (list-push acc e)

  lir-iv-sort : List LirIvl, Integer, Integer, List LirIvl -> List LirIvl
  lir-iv-sort (ivs) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-iv-sort ivs (i + 1) n (lir-iv-insert (list-at ivs i) acc 0 (list-length acc) [])

 Intervals come from a flat walk in list order, which for a DAG is a
 topological order and so a use is always numbered after its definition. A
 back-edge breaks that: the loop's last instructions write the parameters
 that the header, numbered first, reads on the next iteration. Every
 parameter's live range therefore spans the whole loop, and the flat walk
 cannot see it -- it ends each parameter at its last textual use, and the
 allocator would happily hand that register to something else for the rest of
 the body.

 Extending every parameter to one past the last instruction fixes it, and the
 parameters are the only values that need it: in a self-tail-recursive
 function the entire body is the loop and the header is the entry, so the only
 values carried from one iteration to the next are the ones the back-edge
 writes -- the parameters. Everything else is recomputed from them each time
 round. A non-parameter that was somehow live into the header would be outside
 this argument, and the selector's gate refuses such a function rather than
 let this assumption cover it silently.

  lir-iv-extend-params : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-iv-extend-params (fin) (i) (np) (p) =
   if i >= np then fin
   else lir-iv-extend-params (list-set-at fin i p) (i + 1) np p

  lir-build-intervals : LirFunc -> List LirIvl
  lir-build-intervals (f) =
   let nv = f.nvregs
   in let start0 = lir-int-vec 0 nv (0 - 2) []
   in let fin0 = lir-int-vec 0 nv (0 - 2) []
   in let a = lir-iv-blocks (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) start0 fin0 0 nv
   in let start1 = lir-iv-params (a.start) 0 (f.nparams)
   in let fin1 = lir-iv-fin-fix start1 (a.fin) 0 nv
   in let fin2 = lir-iv-result (f.result) fin1 (a.p) nv
   in let fin3 = if lir-has-backedge f then lir-iv-extend-params fin2 0 (f.nparams) (a.p) else fin2
   in let ivs = lir-iv-assemble start1 fin3 0 nv []
   in lir-iv-sort ivs 0 (list-length ivs) []

 The scan. An active entry records a vreg currently held in a register: its
 `fin` (for expiry and for finding the furthest-out victim) and the
 register it holds.

  LirActive = record {
   vreg : Integer,
   fin : Integer,
   reg : Integer
  }

  LirExpire = record {
   active : List LirActive,
   free : List Boolean
  }

  LirScan = record {
   assign : List Location,
   active : List LirActive,
   free : List Boolean,
   nslots : Integer
  }

  lir-true-vec : Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-true-vec (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else lir-true-vec (i + 1) n (list-push acc True)

  lir-loc-init : Integer, Integer, List Location -> List Location
  lir-loc-init (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else lir-loc-init (i + 1) n (list-push acc (deck-record (LocReg lir-alloc-ncallee)))

  lir-free-lowest : List Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-free-lowest (free) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then 0 - 1 else if list-at free i then i else lir-free-lowest free (i + 1) n

 Expire retires every active interval whose `fin` is strictly before the
 new interval's `start`, returning its register to the free pool. The
 boundary is strict (`fin < start`): an interval whose last use is at the
 exact position another's definition begins is kept, so a value and a value
 born where it dies get different registers. This is conservative -- it
 costs a register in the tightest case rather than depending on a
 read-then-overwrite the abstract LIR does not model yet.

  lir-scan-expire : List LirActive, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirActive, List Boolean -> LirExpire
  lir-scan-expire (active) (i) (n) (start) (kept) (free) =
   if i >= n then LirExpire { active = kept, free = free }
   else let a = list-at active i
   in if (a.fin) < start then lir-scan-expire active (i + 1) n start kept (list-set-at free (a.reg) True)
      else lir-scan-expire active (i + 1) n start (list-push kept a) free

 The spill victim is the active interval of greatest `fin` (furthest next
 use), ties broken by greatest vreg. Returns its index in `active`, or -1
 when active is empty.

  lir-scan-victim : List LirActive, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-scan-victim (active) (i) (n) (best) =
   if i >= n then best
   else if best < 0 then lir-scan-victim active (i + 1) n i
   else let a = list-at active i
   in let bb = list-at active best
   in if ((a.fin) > (bb.fin)) | (((a.fin) == (bb.fin)) & ((a.vreg) > (bb.vreg))) then lir-scan-victim active (i + 1) n i
      else lir-scan-victim active (i + 1) n best

  lir-remove-at : List LirActive, Integer, Integer, Integer, List LirActive -> List LirActive
  lir-remove-at (lst) (idx) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else if i == idx then lir-remove-at lst idx (i + 1) n acc
   else lir-remove-at lst idx (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at lst i))

 The flat positions of the call instructions, in the exact numbering the
 intervals use (blocks in list order, every instruction advancing the
 counter by one -- `lir-iv-insn` bumps `p` for every kind, so this walk must
 too, or a crossing test would read the wrong positions). A value live
 strictly across one of these positions cannot sit in a caller-saved
 register.

  LirCallAcc = record {
   positions : List Integer,
   p : Integer
  }

 This walk must mirror `lir-iv-insns` position for position -- two per
 instruction -- or the crossing test compares an interval against a call that
 is somewhere else, and a value live across a call is handed a register the
 call destroys. It is not a wrong number; it is a miscompile.

 A call is recorded at its write position, `p + 1`, which is where it does the
 clobbering. Its own arguments end at `p` and its result begins at `p + 1`, so
 neither is caught by `start < cp & cp < fin`, and both are free to use a
 caller-saved register -- which is the whole point.

  lir-callpos-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> LirCallAcc
  lir-callpos-insns (insns) (i) (len) (positions) (p) =
   if i >= len then LirCallAcc { positions = positions, p = p }
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> lir-callpos-insns insns (i + 1) len (list-push positions (p + 1)) (p + 2)
    is otherwise -> lir-callpos-insns insns (i + 1) len positions (p + 2)

  lir-callpos-blocks : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> LirCallAcc
  lir-callpos-blocks (blocks) (i) (len) (positions) (p) =
   if i >= len then LirCallAcc { positions = positions, p = p }
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let a = lir-callpos-insns (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) positions p
   in lir-callpos-blocks blocks (i + 1) len (a.positions) (a.p)

 Where the divisions are, on the same two-slot numbering and recorded at the
 write slot for the same reason a call is: that is where the fixed registers
 are destroyed. A division's own operands end at the read slot before it and
 its result begins at the write slot, so neither is caught by the crossing
 test -- the dividend is free to be moved into RAX and the remainder is free to
 stay in RDX, which is the entire point.

  lir-divpos-op : LirInsn -> Boolean
  lir-divpos-op (ins) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) ->
     if lir-op-divides op == False then False
     else lir-imm-pow2 r == False
    is otherwise -> False

  lir-divpos-insns : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> LirCallAcc
  lir-divpos-insns (insns) (i) (len) (positions) (p) =
   if i >= len then LirCallAcc { positions = positions, p = p }
   else if lir-divpos-op (list-at insns i)
        then lir-divpos-insns insns (i + 1) len (list-push positions (p + 1)) (p + 2)
        else lir-divpos-insns insns (i + 1) len positions (p + 2)

  lir-divpos-blocks : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> LirCallAcc
  lir-divpos-blocks (blocks) (i) (len) (positions) (p) =
   if i >= len then LirCallAcc { positions = positions, p = p }
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let a = lir-divpos-insns (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) positions p
   in lir-divpos-blocks blocks (i + 1) len (a.positions) (a.p)

  lir-divpos : LirFunc -> List Integer
  lir-divpos (f) =
   (lir-divpos-blocks (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) [] 0).positions

  lir-callpos : LirFunc -> List Integer
  lir-callpos (f) =
   let a = lir-callpos-blocks (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) [] 0
   in a.positions

 An interval crosses a call when a call position lies strictly between its
 start and its fin. Strictness on both ends is deliberate: a value defined
 by the call (`start == cp`, the result arriving in RAX) and a value whose
 last use is the call (`fin == cp`, an argument read before the clobber) are
 both safe in RAX; only a value defined before and used after is not.

  lir-ivl-crosses : Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-ivl-crosses (start) (fin) (callpos) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else let cp = list-at callpos i
   in if (start < cp) & (cp < fin) then True
      else lir-ivl-crosses start fin callpos (i + 1) n

 A non-crosser prefers RAX -- index `lir-alloc-ncallee`, the caller-saved
 register -- and falls back to the lowest free callee register. Preferring
 RAX keeps the callee-saved pool free for the crossers that need it.

 In a leaf that preference is not merely unnecessary, it is backwards. A
 function that makes no call has nothing that can clobber a caller-saved
 register underneath it, so the selector hands it a register file that is
 caller-saved throughout and pushes nothing at all. Every index is then
 equally cheap, and the only thing left worth optimising is *which* register
 a parameter lands in: the selector's leaf file lists the argument registers
 first, in order, so plain lowest-free gives parameter `i` the register it
 already arrived in and the entry moves vanish. Preferring index `ncallee`
 here would scatter that and put the first parameter in RAX.

 The parameters take the lowest indices by construction, not by luck: they
 are the only intervals with `start = -1`, the sort is by start and then by
 vreg, so they are scanned first, in order, against a wholly free file.

 Inside a leaf the choice splits once more, on whether the interval is a
 parameter -- which the scan already knows, because a parameter is exactly an
 interval defined before the first instruction (`start = -1`).

 A parameter wants an argument register, and specifically the one it arrived
 in, so it takes the argument pool and the entry move disappears. Everything
 else wants RAX first: the function's result is copied to RAX before the
 return, so a value that ends up there is returned by an instruction that
 elides. In `sum-to` that is the difference between `mov rdx, rsi` ... `mov
 rax, rdx` and a single `mov rax, rsi`.

 The order matters and only works in this direction. Parameters are scanned
 first, so they claim the argument registers before anything else is placed;
 by the time a non-parameter is scanned, RAX is still free and it takes it.

 A hint is the register a vreg would rather have. It costs nothing to want:
 the scan takes it when it is free and falls back to its ordinary preference
 when it is not, so a hint can never make an allocation worse -- only shorter.

 Both sources of one are a register the value is about to be moved into
 anyway. An argument of a call wants the argument register its position
 requires, which turns `lea rax, [rbx-1]; mov rdi, rax` into `lea rdi,
 [rbx-1]` -- the move elides because the value is already where it is going.
 A call's result wants RAX, where the call has just put it.

 Only a non-crosser is offered its hint. A value live across a call has to be
 callee-saved whatever it would prefer, and every register a hint names is
 caller-saved.

  lir-hint-usable : List Boolean, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-hint-usable (free) (hint) =
   if hint < 0 then False
   else if hint >= lir-alloc-nregs then False
   else list-at free hint

  lir-free-nc : List Boolean, Boolean, Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  lir-free-nc (free) (leaf) (isparam) (hint) =
   if lir-hint-usable free hint then hint
   else if leaf == False then lir-free-nc-call free
   else if isparam then lir-free-leaf-arg free
   else if list-at free lir-alloc-ncallee then lir-alloc-ncallee
   else lir-free-leaf-arg free

 A parameter is never hinted, and the reason is not that it would not help.
 It is that a parameter already arrives somewhere: the entry moves put each
 one from the register it came in to the register it was given, in order, and
 that is only safe while no parameter is going to another parameter's incoming
 register. Hint them and it is not: `f x y = g y x` would want `x` in RSI and
 `y` in RDI, and the entry would emit `mov rsi, rdi` and then read RSI for
 `y` and find `x`. The entry moves would need the same parallel-move
 discipline the argument placement has. They could have it; a parameter that
 is only ever passed straight on is not common enough to have earned it yet.

  lir-hint-set : List Integer, LirValue, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-hint-set (h) (v) (want) (nv) (np) =
   when v
    is LvReg (r) -> if r < np then h else if r >= nv then h else list-set-at h r want
    is LvImm (k) -> h

  lir-hint-args : List LirValue, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-hint-args (args) (h) (i) (n) (nv) (np) =
   if i >= n then h
   else if i >= (lir-alloc-nregs - lir-alloc-nargregs) then h
   else lir-hint-args args (lir-hint-set h (list-at args i) (lir-alloc-nargregs + i) nv np) (i + 1) n nv np

  lir-hint-insns : List LirInsn, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-hint-insns (insns) (h) (i) (n) (nv) (np) =
   if i >= n then h
   else when (list-at insns i)
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) ->
     let h1 = lir-hint-args args h 0 (list-length args) nv np
     in lir-hint-insns insns (lir-hint-set h1 (LvReg dst) lir-alloc-ncallee nv np) (i + 1) n nv np
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) ->
     if lir-imm-pow2 r then lir-hint-insns insns h (i + 1) n nv np
     else when op
      is LoRem -> lir-hint-insns insns (lir-hint-set h (LvReg dst) lir-alloc-divb nv np) (i + 1) n nv np
      is LoDiv -> lir-hint-insns insns (lir-hint-set h (LvReg dst) lir-alloc-diva nv np) (i + 1) n nv np
      is otherwise -> lir-hint-insns insns h (i + 1) n nv np
    is otherwise -> lir-hint-insns insns h (i + 1) n nv np

  lir-hint-blocks : List LirBlock, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-hint-blocks (blocks) (h) (i) (n) (nv) (np) =
   if i >= n then h
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in lir-hint-blocks blocks (lir-hint-insns (b.insns) h 0 (list-length (b.insns)) nv np) (i + 1) n nv np

  lir-hints : LirFunc -> List Integer
  lir-hints (f) =
   lir-hint-blocks (f.blocks) (lir-int-vec 0 (f.nvregs) (0 - 1) []) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) (f.nvregs) (f.nparams)

  lir-free-leaf-arg : List Boolean -> Integer
  lir-free-leaf-arg (free) =
   let fa = lir-free-lowest free lir-alloc-nargregs lir-alloc-nregs
   in if fa >= 0 then fa
      else lir-free-nc-call free

  lir-free-nc-call : List Boolean -> Integer
  lir-free-nc-call (free) =
   if list-at free lir-alloc-ncallee then lir-alloc-ncallee
   else lir-free-lowest free 0 lir-alloc-ncallee

 Place an interval in a free register, or spill it whole to a fresh slot.
 Factored out because the crosser and non-crosser paths share them.

  lir-place-reg : LirIvl, LirScan, List LirActive, List Boolean, Integer -> LirScan
  lir-place-reg (iv) (st) (active) (free) (fr) =
   LirScan {
    assign = list-set-at (st.assign) (iv.vreg) (deck-record (LocReg fr)),
    active = list-push active (deck-record (LirActive { vreg = iv.vreg, fin = iv.fin, reg = fr })),
    free = list-set-at free fr False,
    nslots = st.nslots
   }

  lir-spill-self : LirIvl, LirScan, List LirActive, List Boolean -> LirScan
  lir-spill-self (iv) (st) (active) (free) =
   LirScan { assign = list-set-at (st.assign) (iv.vreg) (deck-record (LocSlot (st.nslots))), active = active, free = free, nslots = st.nslots + 1 }

 The Belady eviction, taken only by a non-crosser that found no free
 register: compare the interval to the furthest-out active victim. If the
 victim reaches further, evict it -- the new interval takes the victim's
 register and the victim goes to a fresh slot; else spill the new interval
 itself. The victim always goes to a slot, never to another register, so a
 crosser (which may only ever be evicted, never placed here) never lands in
 RAX; and only a non-crosser is ever placed here, so RAX is a safe home.

  lir-scan-evict : LirIvl, LirScan, List LirActive, List Boolean -> LirScan
  lir-scan-evict (iv) (st) (active) (free) =
   let vi = lir-scan-victim active 0 (list-length active) (0 - 1)
   in if vi < 0 then lir-spill-self iv st active free
      else let cand = list-at active vi
      in if (cand.fin) > (iv.fin)
         then LirScan {
          assign = list-set-at (list-set-at (st.assign) (cand.vreg) (deck-record (LocSlot (st.nslots)))) (iv.vreg) (deck-record (LocReg (cand.reg))),
          active = list-push (lir-remove-at active vi 0 (list-length active) []) (deck-record (LirActive { vreg = iv.vreg, fin = iv.fin, reg = cand.reg })),
          free = free,
          nslots = st.nslots + 1
         }
         else lir-spill-self iv st active free

 One interval. First expire the intervals that ended before it. Then split
 on whether it crosses a call: a crosser is restricted to the callee-saved
 pool (lowest free in `[0, ncallee)`), spilling itself if none is free; a
 non-crosser prefers RAX, then a callee register, then Belady eviction. This
 is where the fixed-interval constraint the design names becomes real
 without a single special case in the sweep -- it is one branch on a
 precomputed predicate.

 A value live across a division must not sit in either register the division
 fixes. That is narrower than a call, which takes the whole caller-saved pool,
 so it is a mask over the free set rather than a different pool: hide the two
 indices for the duration of the choice and let the ordinary preference pick
 from what remains. A value that crosses a call as well is already confined to
 the callee-saved pool, which the division does not touch.

  lir-free-mask : List Boolean, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Boolean -> List Boolean
  lir-free-mask (free) (a) (b) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= lir-alloc-nregs then acc
   else if (i == a) | (i == b) then lir-free-mask free a b (i + 1) (list-push acc False)
   else lir-free-mask free a b (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at free i))

 The call-crossing arm: a value live across a call needs a callee-saved
 register, so it looks in the low (callee-saved) range and spills itself if
 none is free.

  lir-scan-cross : LirIvl, LirScan, LirExpire -> LirScan
  lir-scan-cross (iv) (st) (ex) =
   let fr = lir-free-lowest (ex.free) 0 lir-alloc-ncallee
   in if fr >= 0 then lir-place-reg iv st (ex.active) (ex.free) fr
      else lir-spill-self iv st (ex.active) (ex.free)

 A value crossing a division cannot use RAX or RDX (idiv destroys them), so
 those are masked out of the free set before the general placement scan.

  lir-scan-avail : LirIvl, LirExpire, List Integer -> List Boolean
  lir-scan-avail (iv) (ex) (divpos) =
   if lir-ivl-crosses (iv.start) (iv.fin) divpos 0 (list-length divpos)
   then lir-free-mask (ex.free) lir-alloc-diva lir-alloc-divb 0 []
   else ex.free

 The non-crossing arm: place into any free register the general scan hands
 out (honouring the hint and the division mask), or evict a lower-priority
 interval when the file is full.

  lir-scan-nocross : LirIvl, LirScan, LirExpire, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> LirScan
  lir-scan-nocross (iv) (st) (ex) (callpos) (hints) (divpos) =
   let avail = lir-scan-avail iv ex divpos
   in let fr = lir-free-nc avail (list-length callpos == 0) (iv.start == 0 - 1) (list-at hints (iv.vreg))
   in if fr >= 0 then lir-place-reg iv st (ex.active) (ex.free) fr
      else lir-scan-evict iv st (ex.active) (ex.free)

 One interval placed. Expire the intervals that ended before this one starts,
 then take the crossing arm if it spans a call, else the general arm. The two
 arms were inline nested lets until a text-emitter round-trip bug on that
 shape forced the split (BACKLOG); they are the same computation.

  lir-scan-step : LirIvl, LirScan, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> LirScan
  lir-scan-step (iv) (st) (callpos) (hints) (divpos) =
   let ex = lir-scan-expire (st.active) 0 (list-length (st.active)) (iv.start) [] (st.free)
   in if lir-ivl-crosses (iv.start) (iv.fin) callpos 0 (list-length callpos)
      then lir-scan-cross iv st ex
      else lir-scan-nocross iv st ex callpos hints divpos

  lir-scan-loop : List LirIvl, Integer, Integer, LirScan, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> LirScan
  lir-scan-loop (ivs) (i) (n) (st) (callpos) (hints) (divpos) =
   if i >= n then st
   else lir-scan-loop ivs (i + 1) n (lir-scan-step (list-at ivs i) st callpos hints divpos) callpos hints divpos

  lir-alloc-linscan : LirFunc -> AllocRes
  lir-alloc-linscan (f) =
   let nv = f.nvregs
   in let ivs = lir-build-intervals f
   in let callpos = lir-callpos f
   in let divpos = lir-divpos f
   in let hints = lir-hints f
   in let st0 = LirScan { assign = lir-loc-init 0 nv [], active = [], free = lir-true-vec 0 lir-alloc-nregs [], nslots = 0 }
   in let stf = lir-scan-loop ivs 0 (list-length ivs) st0 callpos hints divpos
   in AllocRes { assign = stf.assign, ok = True }

Section: Allocation Verifier

 The allocation verifier (LIR.md section 4), distinct from the structural
 one. The structural verifier checks the LOWERING (every vreg defined once,
 used only where defined). This one checks the ALLOCATOR's output: given an
 assignment of each vreg to a physical location, it re-derives what each
 location holds along every path and asserts that every use reads its vreg
 from the location the assignment gave it. It is the checker the design doc
 wants standing BEFORE the allocator, so the allocator is validated from its
 first line -- "validate the result, not the transformer."

 A location is a physical register or a spill slot. Slots are first-class
 from the start (LIR.md section 4: the property that would have caught a
 spill-slot reuse corruption); the linear-scan allocator (Section:
 Allocator) produces both, and this verifier checks both -- `nloc` sizes
 the occupant map to the registers plus however many slots the allocation
 used, so a spilled vreg's slot index is in range.

  Location =
   | LocReg (Integer)
   | LocSlot (Integer)

 The state the verifier carries is a map from location to the vreg it holds,
 one entry per location, `-1` for empty or (at a join of disagreeing paths)
 unknown. Because the LIR is single-assignment along any one path and this
 increment inserts no moves, each location holds at most one vreg; the set
 the design doc describes collapses to a single occupant, and set
 intersection at a join becomes "same vreg on both edges, or unknown". A
 use of vreg V asserts the occupant of V's assigned location is V -- which
 fails exactly when a second vreg was allocated the same register while V
 was still live, the miscompile the verifier exists to catch.

  lir-reg-budget : Integer
  lir-reg-budget = 12

  lir-loc-index : Location, Integer -> Integer
  lir-loc-index (loc) (nregs) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (n) -> n
    is LocSlot (n) -> nregs + n

  lir-loc-text : Location -> Text
  lir-loc-text (loc) =
   when loc
    is LocReg (n) -> "r" & integer-to-text n
    is LocSlot (n) -> "s" & integer-to-text n

 The allocation result: each vreg assigned a `Location`, and an `ok` flag.
 The linear-scan allocator (Section: Allocator) always succeeds -- it
 spills rather than declining -- but the flag is kept for a future target
 whose register file cannot represent some construct.

  AllocRes = record {
   assign : List Location,
   ok : Boolean
  }

 The number of distinct spill slots an assignment uses (the largest slot
 index plus one, or zero if nothing spilled). The verifier sizes its
 occupant map to `lir-reg-budget + this`, so a slot's location index
 (`lir-reg-budget + slot`) is always in range.

  lir-assign-max-slot : List Location, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-assign-max-slot (assign) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else when (list-at assign i)
    is LocSlot (s) -> lir-assign-max-slot assign (i + 1) n (if (s + 1) > acc then s + 1 else acc)
    is LocReg (r) -> lir-assign-max-slot assign (i + 1) n acc

  lir-occ-make : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-occ-make (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else lir-occ-make (i + 1) n (list-push acc (0 - 1))

  lir-occ-copy : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-occ-copy (src) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else lir-occ-copy src (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at src i))

  lir-occ-intersect : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-occ-intersect (a) (b) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else lir-occ-intersect a b (i + 1) n (list-push acc (if (list-at a i) == (list-at b i) then list-at a i else 0 - 1))

  lir-alloc-init-entries : Integer, Integer, List (List Integer) -> List (List Integer)
  lir-alloc-init-entries (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc else lir-alloc-init-entries (i + 1) n (list-push acc [])

  lir-assigned-loc : List Location, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lir-assigned-loc (assign) (vreg) (nregs) =
   if vreg < 0 then 0 - 1 else if vreg >= list-length assign then 0 - 1 else lir-loc-index (list-at assign vreg) nregs

  lir-alloc-seed-params : List Location, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-alloc-seed-params (assign) (i) (np) (nregs) (occ) =
   if i >= np then occ
   else lir-alloc-seed-params assign (i + 1) np nregs (list-set-at occ (lir-loc-index (list-at assign i) nregs) i)

  lir-alloc-use-msg : Text, Integer, Integer, LirValue, List Integer, List Location, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  lir-alloc-use-msg (fname) (bid) (i) (v) (occ) (assign) (nregs) (msgs) =
   when v
    is LvImm (k) -> msgs
    is LvReg (r) ->
     let fl = lir-assigned-loc assign r nregs
     in if fl < 0 then list-push msgs ((fname) & ": alloc use of unassigned vreg " & integer-to-text r)
        else if (list-at occ fl) == r then msgs
        else list-push msgs ((fname) & ": alloc vreg " & integer-to-text r & " not in " & lir-loc-text (list-at assign r) & " at block " & integer-to-text bid & " insn " & integer-to-text i)

  lir-alloc-def : Integer, List Integer, List Location, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-alloc-def (dst) (occ) (assign) (nregs) =
   let fl = lir-assigned-loc assign dst nregs
   in if fl < 0 then occ else list-set-at occ fl dst

 A call clobbers every caller-saved allocatable register (LIR.md section 4).
 In abstract register space those are the indices `[lir-alloc-ncallee,
 lir-alloc-nregs)` -- here the single RAX at index `lir-alloc-ncallee`. Any
 vreg the allocator left there is scrubbed to unknown, so a value the
 allocator wrongly kept in RAX across a call fails its next use here rather
 than only in the emitted bytes. The occupant map is indexed by the abstract
 register number (`lir-loc-index` of a `LocReg` is its index), so this reads
 the allocator's constants, not the verifier's wider `lir-reg-budget`.

  lir-alloc-clobber-cs : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lir-alloc-clobber-cs (occ) (j) (n) =
   if j >= n then occ
   else lir-alloc-clobber-cs (list-set-at occ j (0 - 1)) (j + 1) n

 A division destroys RAX and RDX and nothing else, and the allocator's
 crossing test masks exactly those two out of the free set. The checker
 mirrors that here or it is blind at the one spot the masking protects: a
 value the allocator wrongly kept in RAX or RDX across a division must fail
 its next use. The predicate is the same one `lir-divpos` records positions
 with, so a power-of-two division -- emitted as a shift or mask, destroying
 nothing -- clobbers nothing here either. The division's own result is
 recorded after the scrub, because landing the quotient in RAX or the
 remainder in RDX is legitimate and hinted for.

  lir-alloc-clobber-div : List Integer -> List Integer
  lir-alloc-clobber-div (occ) =
   list-set-at (list-set-at occ lir-alloc-diva (0 - 1)) lir-alloc-divb (0 - 1)

  lir-alloc-use-msg-list : Text, Integer, Integer, List LirValue, List Integer, List Location, Integer, List Text, Integer, Integer -> List Text
  lir-alloc-use-msg-list (fname) (bid) (ii) (args) (occ) (assign) (nregs) (msgs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then msgs
   else lir-alloc-use-msg-list fname bid ii args occ assign nregs (lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid ii (list-at args i) occ assign nregs msgs) (i + 1) n

  LirAllocWalk = record {
   occ : List Integer,
   msgs : List Text
  }

  lir-alloc-check-insns : Text, Integer, List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Location, Integer, List Text -> LirAllocWalk
  lir-alloc-check-insns (fname) (bid) (insns) (i) (len) (occ) (assign) (nregs) (msgs) =
   if i >= len then LirAllocWalk { occ = occ, msgs = msgs }
   else let ins = list-at insns i
   in when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) ->
     let m1 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i l occ assign nregs msgs
     in let m2 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i r occ assign nregs m1
     in let occ1 = if lir-divpos-op ins then lir-alloc-clobber-div occ else occ
     in lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len (lir-alloc-def dst occ1 assign nregs) assign nregs m2
    is LiMove (dst) (src) ->
     let m1 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i src occ assign nregs msgs
     in lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len (lir-alloc-def dst occ assign nregs) assign nregs m1
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) ->
     let m1 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i base occ assign nregs msgs
     in lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len (lir-alloc-def dst occ assign nregs) assign nregs m1
    is LiCmp (l) (r) ->
     let m1 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i l occ assign nregs msgs
     in let m2 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i r occ assign nregs m1
     in lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len occ assign nregs m2
    is LiTest (l) (r) ->
     let m1 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i l occ assign nregs msgs
     in let m2 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i r occ assign nregs m1
     in lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len occ assign nregs m2
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) ->
     let m1 = lir-alloc-use-msg-list fname bid i args occ assign nregs msgs 0 (list-length args)
     in let occ1 = lir-alloc-clobber-cs occ lir-alloc-ncallee lir-alloc-nregs
     in lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len (lir-alloc-def dst occ1 assign nregs) assign nregs m1
    is LiRet (v) ->
     let m1 = lir-alloc-use-msg fname bid i v occ assign nregs msgs
     in lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len occ assign nregs m1
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len occ assign nregs msgs
    is LiJump (b) -> lir-alloc-check-insns fname bid insns (i + 1) len occ assign nregs msgs

  AllocPropRes = record {
   entries : List (List Integer),
   seen : List Boolean
  }

  lir-alloc-propagate : List (List Integer), List Boolean, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> AllocPropRes
  lir-alloc-propagate (entries) (seen) (exit) (succs) (i) (len) (nloc) =
   if i >= len then AllocPropRes { entries = entries, seen = seen }
   else let sid = list-at succs i
   in if (list-at seen sid) == False
      then lir-alloc-propagate (list-set-at entries sid (lir-occ-copy exit 0 nloc [])) (list-set-at seen sid True) exit succs (i + 1) len nloc
      else lir-alloc-propagate (list-set-at entries sid (lir-occ-intersect (list-at entries sid) exit 0 nloc [])) seen exit succs (i + 1) len nloc

  LirAllocState = record {
   msgs : List Text,
   last-occ : List Integer
  }

  lir-alloc-check-blocks : Text, List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, List (List Integer), List Boolean, List Location, Integer, Integer, List Text, List Integer -> LirAllocState
  lir-alloc-check-blocks (fname) (blocks) (i) (len) (entries) (seen) (assign) (nregs) (nloc) (msgs) (last-occ) =
   if i >= len then LirAllocState { msgs = msgs, last-occ = last-occ }
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in let running = lir-occ-copy (list-at entries (b.id)) 0 nloc []
   in let w = lir-alloc-check-insns fname (b.id) (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) running assign nregs msgs
   in let succs = lir-block-succs (b.insns)
   in let prop = lir-alloc-propagate entries seen (w.occ) succs 0 (list-length succs) nloc
   in lir-alloc-check-blocks fname blocks (i + 1) len (prop.entries) (prop.seen) assign nregs nloc (w.msgs) (w.occ)

  lir-alloc-result-msg : Text, LirValue, List Integer, List Location, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  lir-alloc-result-msg (fname) (res) (occ) (assign) (nregs) (msgs) =
   when res
    is LvImm (k) -> msgs
    is LvReg (r) ->
     let fl = lir-assigned-loc assign r nregs
     in if fl < 0 then list-push msgs ((fname) & ": alloc result vreg " & integer-to-text r & " unassigned")
        else if (list-at occ fl) == r then msgs
        else list-push msgs ((fname) & ": alloc result vreg " & integer-to-text r & " not in " & lir-loc-text (list-at assign r) & " at exit")

  lir-alloc-check : LirFunc, List Location -> List Text
  lir-alloc-check (f) (assign) =
   let nb = list-length (f.blocks)
   in let nregs = lir-reg-budget
   in let nloc = nregs + lir-assign-max-slot assign 0 (list-length assign) 0
   in let entries0 = lir-alloc-init-entries 0 nb []
   in let occ0 = lir-alloc-seed-params assign 0 (f.nparams) nregs (lir-occ-make 0 nloc [])
   in let entries1 = list-set-at entries0 0 occ0
   in let seen1 = list-set-at (lir-make-vec 0 nb []) 0 True
   in let st = lir-alloc-check-blocks (f.name) (f.blocks) 0 nb entries1 seen1 assign nregs nloc [] (lir-occ-make 0 nloc [])
   in lir-alloc-result-msg (f.name) (f.result) (st.last-occ) assign nregs (st.msgs)

Section: Dump and Pass

 A textual rendering of a lowered function: the signature, the vreg count,
 the blocks (each `bN:` then its instructions, joined by ` | `), and the
 result operand. It exists so the lowering can be driven over real IR and
 read back from the compile log, before any allocator consumes it — the
 same way the occurrence analyser's `occ-report` proved that analysis over
 real lowered IR.

  lir-val-text : LirValue -> Text
  lir-val-text (v) =
   when v
    is LvReg (r) -> "v" & integer-to-text r
    is LvImm (k) -> "#" & integer-to-text k

  lir-op-text : LirOp -> Text
  lir-op-text (op) =
   when op
    is LoAdd -> "add"
    is LoSub -> "sub"
    is LoMul -> "mul"
    is LoRem -> "rem"
    is LoDiv -> "div"

  lir-cond-text : LirCond -> Text
  lir-cond-text (c) =
   when c
    is LcEq -> "eq"
    is LcNe -> "ne"
    is LcLt -> "lt"
    is LcGt -> "gt"
    is LcLe -> "le"
    is LcGe -> "ge"

  lir-args-text : List LirValue, Integer, Integer -> Text
  lir-args-text (args) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then "" else " " & lir-val-text (list-at args i) & lir-args-text args (i + 1) n

  lir-insn-text : LirInsn -> Text
  lir-insn-text (ins) =
   when ins
    is LiBin (op) (dst) (l) (r) -> "v" & integer-to-text dst & " = " & lir-op-text op & " " & lir-val-text l & " " & lir-val-text r
    is LiMove (dst) (src) -> "v" & integer-to-text dst & " = mov " & lir-val-text src
    is LiLoad (dst) (base) (off) (w) (sg) -> "v" & integer-to-text dst & " = load " & lir-val-text base & " +" & integer-to-text off & " w" & integer-to-text w & (if sg then "s" else "")
    is LiCmp (l) (r) -> "cmp " & lir-val-text l & " " & lir-val-text r
    is LiTest (l) (r) -> "test " & lir-val-text l & " " & lir-val-text r
    is LiRet (v) -> "ret " & lir-val-text v
    is LiCall (dst) (name) (args) -> "v" & integer-to-text dst & " = call " & name & lir-args-text args 0 (list-length args)
    is LiBranch (c) (bt) (be) -> "br " & lir-cond-text c & " b" & integer-to-text bt & " b" & integer-to-text be
    is LiJump (b) -> "jmp b" & integer-to-text b

  lir-insns-text : List LirInsn, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  lir-insns-text (insns) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else lir-insns-text insns (i + 1) len (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else "; ") & lir-insn-text (list-at insns i))

  lir-block-text : LirBlock -> Text
  lir-block-text (b) =
   "b" & integer-to-text (b.id) & ":" & (if list-length (b.insns) == 0 then "" else " " & lir-insns-text (b.insns) 0 (list-length (b.insns)) "")

  lir-blocks-text : List LirBlock, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  lir-blocks-text (blocks) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else lir-blocks-text blocks (i + 1) len (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else " | ") & lir-block-text (list-at blocks i))

  lir-dump-func : LirFunc -> Text
  lir-dump-func (f) =
   "LIR " & (f.name) & "(" & integer-to-text (f.nparams) & ") nvregs=" & integer-to-text (f.nvregs) & ": " & lir-blocks-text (f.blocks) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) "" & " => " & lir-val-text (f.result)

 A textual rendering of the block-level liveness: each block (in list order,
 the same order the LIR dump prints) with the set of vregs live on entry.
 The result vreg shows up live at the exit block, an incoming parameter at
 the entry. It is emitted alongside the LIR dump under `-Passes lir-dump`.

  lir-liveset-text : List Boolean, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  lir-liveset-text (vec) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-liveset-text vec (i + 1) n (if list-at vec i then (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else ",") & "v" & integer-to-text i) else acc)

  lir-liveblocks-text : List LirBlock, List (List Boolean), Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  lir-liveblocks-text (blocks) (livein) (i) (n) (nv) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else let b = list-at blocks i
   in lir-liveblocks-text blocks livein (i + 1) n nv (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else " | ") & "b" & integer-to-text (b.id) & " in {" & lir-liveset-text (list-at livein (b.id)) 0 nv "" & "}")

  lir-liveness-text : LirFunc -> Text
  lir-liveness-text (f) =
   "LIVE " & (f.name) & ": " & lir-liveblocks-text (f.blocks) (lir-liveness f) 0 (list-length (f.blocks)) (f.nvregs) ""

 A textual rendering of the linear-scan allocation and its verification:
 each vreg with the location it was assigned (a register `rN` or a spill
 slot `sN`), then the verifier's verdict. The assignment now shows register
 reuse (two vregs with disjoint live ranges sharing a register) and, under
 pressure, spill slots. The verifier runs over this real allocated code on
 every dump, and the same verifier catches a wrong allocation (proven by
 corrupting the assignment).

  lir-assign-text : List Location, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  lir-assign-text (assign) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else lir-assign-text assign (i + 1) n (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else " ") & "v" & integer-to-text i & "->" & lir-loc-text (list-at assign i))

  lir-alloc-dump : LirFunc -> Text
  lir-alloc-dump (f) =
   let a = lir-alloc-linscan f
   in let viol = lir-alloc-check f (a.assign)
   in "ALLOC " & (f.name) & ": " & lir-assign-text (a.assign) 0 (f.nvregs) "" & (if list-length viol == 0 then " [alloc-check: ok]" else " [alloc-check: " & integer-to-text (list-length viol) & " violations]")

 The opt-in pass. For each definition it lowers to the LIR as far as the
 whitelist allows; a lowerable function reports its dump and any structural
 violations, and a function outside the whitelist reports that it was not
 lowered. The lines ride the info channel, the same as `occ-report`, so
 `-Passes lir-dump` reports without halting and still emits a binary.

  lir-report-chapter : IRChapter -> List Text
  lir-report-chapter (ch) = lir-report-defs (ch.defs) 0 (list-length (ch.defs)) []

 The post-resolve entry point. `lir-dump` names itself in the pass
 pipeline, but a constructor pattern needs resolved types, so the report is
 produced after the RESOLVE and LIFT phases rather than inside the
 pre-resolve pipeline. `opening.codex` calls this over the final
 `cdx-chapter`; when the pipeline does not request `lir-dump` it returns the
 empty list and costs nothing.

  lir-pipeline-wants : List Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lir-pipeline-wants (names) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then False
   else if list-at names i == "lir-dump" then True
   else lir-pipeline-wants names (i + 1) n

  lir-report-if : List Text, IRChapter -> List Text
  lir-report-if (pipeline) (ch) =
   if lir-pipeline-wants pipeline 0 (list-length pipeline) then lir-report-chapter ch else []

  lir-report-defs : List IRDef, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  lir-report-defs (defs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in let r = lower-def-to-lir d
   in if r.ok == False then lir-report-defs defs (i + 1) len (list-push acc ("LIR " & (d.name) & ": (not in the LIR whitelist)"))
      else let viol = lir-check-func (r.func)
      in let line = lir-dump-func (r.func) & (if list-length viol == 0 then " [check: ok]" else " [check: " & integer-to-text (list-length viol) & " violations]")
      in let live-line = lir-liveness-text (r.func)
      in let alloc-line = lir-alloc-dump (r.func)
      in lir-report-defs defs (i + 1) len (append-violations (list-push (list-push (list-push acc line) live-line) alloc-line) viol 0 (list-length viol))

  append-violations : List Text, List Text, Integer, Integer -> List Text
  append-violations (acc) (viol) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else append-violations (list-push acc (list-at viol i)) viol (i + 1) len
